  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/23538904078773066103">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">135</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/tsvangirai_morgan_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-06</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 16, 2009
. Robert G. Mugabe Morgan Tsvangirai is the leader of Zimbabwe's main opposition party, the Movement for Democratic Change (MDC). He was sworn in as Zimbabwe's prime minister on February 11 following months of wrangling over the terms of a power-sharing agreement with longtime nemesis President
Mr. Tsvangirai formed his party in 1999 with union workers, rich white farmers and ethnic Ndebeles from Zimbabwe's southwest. The next year, it won almost half of the contested primary seats in parliamentary elections. Mr. Tsvangirai narrowly lost the presidential race in 2002 in an election that observers denounced as either flawed or fraudulent. He was charged and acquitted with plotting to assassinate President Mugabe, a charge that carried a possible death penalty, and was beaten severely by state security forces.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-01</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Tsvangirai, Morgan</skos:prefLabel>
