    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Daschle, Tom</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/d/tom_daschle/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/daschle_tom_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-30</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 24, 2009
Tom Daschle, the former South Dakota senator, was nominated by President Barack Obama to be secretary of health and human services. He withdrew as the nominee on Feb. 3, 2009, because he had failed to pay more than $128,000 in taxes.
Nonetheless, with unrivaled ties to both Capitol Hill and the White House, he has remained an influential player in the debate over a health care overhaul. Mr. Daschle speaks frequently to President Obama, and many of the president's top advisers were once on Mr. Daschle's staff. And he is still a highly paid policy adviser to hospital, drug, pharmaceutical and other health care industry clients of Alston &amp; Bird, the law and lobbying firm.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.tom_daschle"/>
  </rdf:Description>
