    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/mickelson_phil_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 16, 2009
Phil Mickelson is one of the top professional golfers in the world. He has won 38 PGA tournaments in his career, including three major championships: the 2004 and 2006 Masters and the 2005 PGA championship. He is known for an outstanding short game, booming drives off the tee, and the occasionally bewildering shot that throws caution to the wind when playing the percentages will do.
He left the PGA tour in May of 2009 for several weeks immediately after his wife Amy was diagnosed with breast cancer. Doctors believe they found the disease early, and Phil returned to the tour in June. The Mickelsons married in 1996 and they have three young children, Amanda, Sophia and Evan.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-12</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Mickelson, Phil</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-09</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
