  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/86937731761950235273">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">When he was first elected, in 1994, after serving three terms in Congress, John G. Rowland was the youngest governor in the nation. But in his third term as governor of Connecticut, he was investigated for having lied about work done to his Litchfield vacation cottage by people working for and doing business with the state.
Mr. Rowland resigned his office in June of 2004. Lt. Gov. M. Jodi Rell took over his post.
Mr. Rowland was released in February 2006 from a federal prison camp where he served more than 10 months after pleading guilty to corruption charges.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/r/john_g_rowland/index.html"/>
