    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 21, 2009
Representative Jane Harman, a moderate from Southern California, was one of the Democratic Party's most outspoken voices on national security matters after the Sept. 11 attacks. Ms. Harman became the ranking Democrat on the House Intelligence Committee after the 2002 election; she campaigned to become chairwoman when the party gained control of the House in 2006, but was passed over by Speaker Nancy Pelosi.
agreeing to seek lenient treatment from the Bush administration for the lobbyists. One official who has seen transcripts of several wiretapped calls said she appeared to agree to intercede in exchange for help in persuading party leaders to give her the intelligence committee chair. National Security Agency In April 2009, it was revealed that Ms. Harman had been caught up in circumstances that appeared to link that ambition and an espionage investigation involving two pro-Israel lobbyists. Current and former government officials said Ms. Harman had been overheard on telephone calls intercepted by the</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
