    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">22</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/f/kenneth_r_feinberg/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-12-14</nyt:first_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/feinberg_kenneth_r_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 22, 2009
Kenneth R. Feinberg was appointed by the Obama administration on June 10, 2009, as compensation overseer with broad discretion to set the pay for 175 top executives at seven of the nation's largest companies, which have received hundreds of billions of dollars in federal assistance to survive.
He is a well-known Washington lawyer and mediator whose last high-profile assignment was putting a financial value on the lives of victims of the 9/11 attack.
Mr. Feinberg's plan, announced in October 2009, will cut total compensation by about 50 percent for 25 top earners at seven companies that received exceptional help. The companies affected are Citigroup, Bank of America, American International Group, General Motors, Chrysler and the financing arms of the two automakers.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
