  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N19036171254988906693">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">22</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-11</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/zhao_ziyang_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Zhao Ziyang</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 14, 2009
Zhao Ziyang, a former general secretary of China's Communist Party, was stripped of power for supporting the students during their 1989 pro-democracy Tiananmen Square protests. He died in January 2005.  
From 1989 until his death, he lived under house arrest not far from the government offices where he once led China.
, the patriarch behind the scenes. Deng Xiaoping In a secretly recorded memoir published in book form in May 2009 as  "Prisoner of the State: The Secret Journal of Premier Zhao Ziyang" (Simon and Schuster), he recalled that as he feuded with hard-line party rivals over how to handle the students occupying Tiananmen Square, he requested a personal audience with</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/z/zhao_ziyang/index.html"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-17</nyt:latest_use>
