  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N44254751710817044863">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-19</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 18, 2009
By Choe Sang-Hun
Kim Dae-jung, a dissident who survived a death sentence and an assassination attempt by military dictators before winning the South Korean presidency and receiving a Nobel Peace Prize, died on Aug. 18, 2009. He was 83.
As president from 1998 till 2003, Mr. Kim was the first opposition leader to take power in South Korea.
Once vilified by his rivals as a Communist, Mr. Kim flew to Pyongyang, the capital of North Korea, in 2000 to meet that nation's leader, Kim Jong-il, in the first summit meeting between the Koreas. That meeting led to an unprecedented détente on the divided Korean Peninsula, which remains technically at war because no peace treaty was signed at the end of the Korean War in 1953.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-09-05</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/_kim_dae_jung/index.html"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Kim Dae Jung</skos:prefLabel>
