    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 28, 2009
By Jon Pareles
Marshall Mathers is better known as the multimilllion-selling rapper Eminem. In late December 2007, a depressed, writer's-blocked, pill-popping, opiate-addicted Mr. Mathers overdosed on some new blue pills someone gave him -- they were methadone -- and collapsed on his bathroom floor. Public statements covered up the reason for his emergency hospitalization and detox, claiming the problem was pneumonia. A month later he had ramped up his habit again.
But the overdose scared him. Early in 2008, he hospitalized himself, went through rehab and started the full 12-step program of a recovering addict, complete with meetings, a sponsor and a therapist. Mr. Mathers says he has stayed sober since April 20, 2008.</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Eminem</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
