  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N41239414179219975573">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Barak, Ehud</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">41</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.ehud_barak"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 25, 2009
Ehud Barak is the Israeli defense minister. He had previously served as prime minister and as army chief of staff and is Israel's most decorated soldier.
As a young commando, Mr. Barak placed a woman's wig on his head, squeezed into high heels and stuffed an oversized purse with explosives as he set off on a derring-do -- and ultimately successful -- mission to eliminate a Palestinian terrorist cell in Beirut.
As prime minister and leader of the Labor Party, Mr. Barak in late 2000 went further down the road toward a final peace settlement with Yasir Arafat, the Palestinian leader, than any other Israeli leader before or since. His willingness to offer concessions that went beyond what many Israelis were prepared for, and Mr. Arafat's last-minute rejection of a sweeping deal brokered by President Bill Clinton left Mr. Barak vulnerable, and he was swept from office by Ariel Sharon in elections in 2001.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/barak_ehud_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-12</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
