  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N13941567618952269073">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">109</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 8, 2009
Michelle Obama is the first African-American to assume the role of First Lady.
The wife of President Obama has said she dislikes politics, but over the course of a grueling two-year campaign she emerged as a political presence all her own. And in the early days of the Obama administration she made direct forays into the world of policy, promoting bills that support her husband's agenda.
She has become one of the Obama administration's most visible surrogates on health care, announcing the release of $851 million in federal financing for health clinics, calling for tougher nutritional standards in the government's school lunch program and urging Democrats to rally around the president's efforts to revamp health care.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/o/michelle_obama/index.html"/>
