  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/50711559888164634213">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-27</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/penn_irving_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Penn, Irving</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/p/irving_penn/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 7, 2009
's lithe body in layers of winter clothing. His subjects, who included many of the greatest creative talents of the 20th century, emerged from their portrait sessions with their carefully shaped personas profoundly shaken. Mr. Penn died on Oct. 7, 2009; he was 92. Rudolf Nureyev  in a corner, exposed Colette's forehead and swaddled  Marcel Duchamp The photographer Irving Penn put
As one of the 20th century's most prolific and influential photographers of fashion and the famous, Mr. Penn's signature blend of classical elegance and cool minimalism was recognizable to magazine readers and museumgoers worldwide.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">8</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-04-28</nyt:first_use>
