    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/chambers_robert_e_jr_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-04-22</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">12</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/c/robert_e_chambers_jr/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Chambers, Robert E Jr</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">When Robert E. Chambers Jr., then 19, was arrested in the death of Jennifer Levin, 18, he was immediately branded ?The Preppy Killer? by the tabloid press.
The case carried heady themes of sex, drinking and underage youth gone wild. The son of a nurse, who struggled to send him to expensive prep schools, Mr. Chambers was expelled from college and developed a lifelong addiction to drugs. At 6 feet 4 inches, with an athletic build, he was attractive to many women.
On Aug. 26, 1986, Ms. Levin?s body was discovered in Central Park behind the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The daughter of a real estate broker and an advertising executive, Ms. Levin had been sexually abused and strangled after a night of drinking at an Upper East Side bar.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-07-13</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
