  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N32469620840257956203">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.kirk_kerkorian"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/kerkorian_kirk_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-26</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 11, 2009
Kirk Kerkorian has made billions on brazen investments in Las Vegas and Hollywood.
His fortune appears to have peaked in 2007 when his stake in the casino giant MGM Mirage was worth about $14 billion. In June 2009, the value of all his public holdings is less than $2 billion.
Over his long career, Mr. Kerkorian has focused largely on gaming, movies and automobile investments, at times spinning off assets only to snap them up from those in distress later, showing little desire to diversify.
For years, Detroit seemed to be where his heart was. Mr. Kerkorian tried twice to buy Chrysler; he also made a large investment in General Motors and tried to use that stake as a lever to change the giant company's direction. On April 28, 2008, he announced he was seeking a major share of Ford, through purchases made by the Tracinda Corporation, his investment arm.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-06-11</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/kirk_kerkorian/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">52</nyt:associated_article_count>
