  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N71333521099232962763">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/f/jane_fonda/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">While the rest of us have just about managed one life, Jane Fonda has had half a dozen. She has been a sex kitten, a fashion model, a radical and war protester, an Oscar-winning movie star, an exercise impresario and the consort of a billionaire. Her marital history alone has made her a kind of cultural bellwether. Her first husband, the French director Roger Vadim, introduced her to threesomes; she first made love with her second husband, Tom Hayden, after he showed her some slides of Vietnamese peasants (this was back when people took foreplay seriously); and her third husband, Ted Turner, told her on their first date, "I have friends who are Communists."</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-04-26</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/fonda_jane_per"/>
