  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N1469616322603670713">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">42</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BYanukovich%2C+Viktor+F%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 23, 2009
Viktor F. Yanukovich is a former prime minister of Ukraine and the leader of its opposition.
Mr. Yanukovich, who has good relations with the Kremlin, was the loser in the country's 2005 Orange Revolution, in which massive peaceful demonstration led to the installation of a pro-Western regime after a disputed election. But he has maintained a power base in the Russian-speaking eastern part of the country.
In the spring of 2009, Prime Minister Yulia V. Tymoshenko, a leader of the Orange Revolution, briefly worked to create a coalition with her former foe against her former ally, President Viktor A. Yushchenko. The effort ended in charges and counter-charges of bad faith.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/yanukovich_viktor_f_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-22</nyt:first_use>
