    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/mcauliffe_terry_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/m/terry_mcauliffe/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">McAuliffe, Terry</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 10, 2009
Terry McAuliffe is the former chairman of the Democratic National Committee and a longtime confidant of Bill and Hillary Clinton. He was one of Mrs. Clinton's closest allies and loudest cheerleaders during the 2008 presidential primary.
He was defeated in his bid to become his party's nominee for governor of Virginia in June 2009, when he lost to State Senator R. Creigh Deeds in an unusually contentious, expensive and closely watched primary for governor.
Mr. McAuliffe had held an early lead in statewide polls based largely on his ability to out-talk and outspend his opponents. But his super-caffeinated style, dependence on out-of-state donors and lack of experience in state politics undercut his popularity among some voters and undermined his claim that his national stature made him the man to beat Robert F. McDonnell, the Republican nominee.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">20</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.terry_mcauliffe"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-14</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
