  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/29187326037058566303">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Lin, Maya</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/l/maya_lin/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">11</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-04</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-08-14</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 7, 2009
A year before the artist and architect Maya Lin was born, her Chinese immigrant parents moved to Athens, Ohio. Her father, Henry Huan Lin, a ceramist, would later become the dean of Ohio University's College of Fine Arts; her mother, Julia Ming Lin, would become a literature professor there. Their daughter, while still an undergraduate at Yale, would design one of America's most striking monuments, the Vietnam Veterans Memorial in Washington.
Ms. Lin, who was born in 1959, is now more artist than architect. She is nearly three decades removed from her "Queen of Death" days, a sobriquet from her university days at Yale and the fame of winning the commission for the memorial, which led to a few more memorials. Ms. Lin has given herself over to the forces of nature; her environmentalist visions reflect the unbroken flow of land and sea, water and wind. Her installations are scattered throughout the country.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.maya_lin"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/lin_maya_per"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
