    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-02-24</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">17</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated April 16, 2009
Archbishop Timothy M. Dolan was named the 10th archbishop of Milwaukee by Pope John Paul II on June 25, 2002. On Feb. 23, 2009, he was named by Pope Benedict XVI to succeed Cardinal Edward M. Egan as the Roman Catholic archbishop of New York.
He was officially inaugurated on April 15, in a three-hour liturgy of installation at St. Patrick's Cathedral that displayed all the magisterial ritual and pageantry in the arsenal of the Catholic Church. In his first homily in the job, he showed some of the diplomatic skill for which he is known, asserting his view of his central role in New York, but with a newcomer's deference.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
