  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/14695991252211157983">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 19, 2009
Don Hewitt changed the course of broadcast news by creating the television magazine "60 Minutes," fusing journalism and show business as never before, and then presided over the much-copied program for nearly four decades. He died on Aug. 19, 2009 at age 86.
space missions of the late 1960's. NASA  in 1960; the assassination of Mr. Kennedy in 1963; and the  John F. Kennedy  and  Richard M. Nixon  between  presidential debate 's coverage of such watershed moments as the  CBS , and presided over  Walter Cronkite During a career at CBS News that lasted more than half a century, Mr. Hewitt served as a living bridge - from the birth of television journalism in the long shadow of radio, through its golden-age as an unrivaled fixture in dens and family rooms, to its middle-age present, under siege from the Internet. As a director and producer, Mr. Hewitt helped shape the early broadcasts of pioneers like Edward R. Murrow, Douglas Edwards and</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-20</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-10</nyt:first_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/hewitt_don_per"/>
