    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/c/elvis_costello/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Elvis Costello could never be accused of thinking small. Starting with an assumed name that evokes the king of rock-and-roll himself, Costello has spent much of his career exploring ways of merging his dour, post-punk point of view with grander, more traditional musical forms without compromising his anti-establishment image.
? Stephen Holden Costello's verbal overkill, his fondness for multisyllable words and offbeat images, often seem like a defense against more direct expression, a sign of the emotional evasiveness that runs through all his work. The denser the language, the more it recasts feelings as attitudes and turns stories into puzzles. In fact one has the impression that Costello is a mystery writer at heart: he would probably make a terrific author of whodunits. If they were anything like his songs, they would be elaborate stories with labyrinthine plots and many desperate characters getting tangled up in riddles as they try to explain themselves.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-06-12</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
