  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N40970110145339386293">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/w/bob_woodward/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Bob Woodward is an award-winning reporter and writer, the author of more than a dozen books about Washington and politics, who became famous because of the Watergate scandal ? shorthand for the revealed abuses of power by the Nixon White House, including illegal wiretapping, burglaries and money laundering.
Mr. Woodward was a young reporter for The Washington Post in June 1972 when he and a colleague, Carl Bernstein, began reporting on what seemed to have been a simple break-in at the Democratic Party?s national offices at the Watergate complex in Washington. In a series of articles for which The Washington Post won a Pulitzer Prize, Mr. Woodward, a former Navy lieutenant, and Mr. Bernstein soon discovered that it was something more ? an attempt by White House officials to wiretap the offices, and an attempt to cover up what had been done.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-10-19</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/woodward_bob_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">40</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-10</nyt:first_use>
