  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N88166935704339931723">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In nearly 20 years in Los Angeles, Anthony Pellicano made himself into the rightful owner of the breathless title, "Detective to the Stars," the one man who would, and seemingly could, do anything to clean up any mess.
Executives and actors, studio bosses and their jilted spouses, the hottest and the has-been: all went to Mr. Pellicano when their situations seemed too complex, or the stakes too high, to leave anything to chance.
When federal agents raided Mr. Pellicano's office in November 2002, his case became a local obsession: who would be fingered next, people wondered anxiously, as investigators gathered evidence and listened to Mr. Pellicano's wiretap tapes.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">57</nyt:associated_article_count>
