    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 9, 2009
The actor Jeremy Piven is best known for his Emmy-winning role as the bombastic, catchphrase-generating superagent Ari Gold on HBO's comedy "Entourage."
He has worked steadily -- if, until ''Entourage,'' without much fanfare -- in Hollywood for two decades. He has a cultivated self-awareness about him that suggests what he really is: a lifelong stage actor, an insider's insider.
through the Actors' Equity union. An arbitrator later ruled in the actor's favor. In testimony, Mr. Piven's doctor said the actor had the Epstein-Barr virus and high levels of mercury. pursued grievance and arbitration hearings 's Hollywood drama "Speed-the-Plow," citing health concerns caused by mercury poisoning. He became the butt of late-night television jokes and was widely ridiculed as a hard-partying Hollywood dilettante who was not up to eight performances a week. The play's producers  David Mamet  by bolting the revival of  stunned the theater world In December 2008 Mr. Piven</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/piven_jeremy_per"/>
