  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/43965769562908368213">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Abraham Lincoln was the 16th president of the United States. He was born in Hodgenville, Ky., in 1809 in a log cabin and accumulated barely a year of formal education while growing up. Family moves took him to Indiana and then to Illinois by the time he was 21. A failed storekeeper, Lincoln worked at odd jobs while he taught himself law, sometimes walking 20 miles to borrow books.
He was elected to the Illinois state legislature as a Whig in 1834 and to Congress in 1846. He unsuccessfully ran for the Senate in 1858, drawing national attention in debates with Stephen A. Douglas, the nation's leading Democrat. He was rewarded with the Republican Party's nomination for president in 1860, and defeated three opponents to win the general election.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/l/abraham_lincoln/index.html"/>
