  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N24842346197830039553">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-09</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Martin Heidegger, born in Messkirch, Germany, in 1889; died in 1976. Philosopher. Generally regarded as a founder of existentialism, he disliked the attribution. He was influenced by the phenomenology of Edmund Husserl. His major work is "Being and Time" (1927). His concern was with the problem of being; he investigated aspects of human existence and the individual's relationship to death; he attributed the difficulties of society to individuals' awareness of their own temporality. He was a supporter of Hitler during the latter's early years in power, and as a result was banned from teaching after World War II.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/heidegger_martin_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
