  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N74532791318055730293">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/hawking_stephen_w_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-17</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 20, 2009
Stephen W. Hawking is a theoretical physicist who pioneered a new approach to the deepest problem of modern science -- explaining the origin and evolution of the universe. But he is as well known for his ability to put the most abstruse scientific questions into language that appeals to nonscientists, and for his struggles against amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, the wasting condition also known as Lou Gehrig's disease.
Since 1963, the condition gradually reduced his bodily control to the flexing of a finger and voluntary eye movements. But while it ravaged his body, it left his mental faculties untouched. By an effort of will that awed his friends and colleagues, Dr. Hawking kept up a busy professional and personal life, refining his often startling theories about the universe, traveling around the globe to scientific meetings, writing a best-selling book about his work, marrying twice, fathering three children and indulging his taste for good food and fine wine.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">13</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/h/stephen_w_hawking/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hawking, Stephen W</skos:prefLabel>
