  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/91653044827073522423">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/b/joshua_b_bolten/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Joshua B. Bolten was named as President Bush's chief of staff in March 2006, as complaints mounted among Republicans that the president's falling popularity and blunders like the bungled response to Hurricane Katrina could cost them control of Congress in the November elections. Many were calling for a major shakeup of the White House staff, beginning with the ouster of Andrew H. Card, who had served as chief of staff since Mr. Bush took office, and an infusion of new blood.
Mr. Bolten did orchestrate a series of personnel changes, but hardly the shakeup many were looking far. And he hardly qualified as new blood, having worked for Mr. Bush from early in his first presidential campaign. He also represented a thoroughly insider point of view.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/bolten_joshua_b_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Bolten, Joshua B</skos:prefLabel>
