  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N88011041517649108453">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Jesse Helms, the former North Carolina Senator whose courtly manner and mossy drawl barely masked a hard-edged conservatism that opposed civil rights, gay rights, foreign aid and modern art, died July 4, 2008. He was 86.
In a 52-year political career that ended with his retirement from the Senate in 2002, Mr. Helms became a beacon for the right wing of American politics, a lightning rod for the left, and, often, a mighty pain for Presidents whatever their political leaning.
Ronald Reagan, a friend who could thank Mr. Helms for critical campaign help, once described him as a ?thorn in my side.? Mr. Helms was known for taking on anyone, even leaders of his own party, who strayed from his idea of ideological purity.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-05</nyt:first_use>
