  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N39599090699283981773">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">66</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Sept. 8, 2009
John Ashcroft is a former attorney general in the Bush administration who played a central role in the war on terror.
After stepping down from the Justice Department in 2005, Mr. Ashcroft opened a Washington consulting and lobbying firm that bears his name.
As the nation's chief law enforcement officer, Mr. Ashcroft played a key role in the Bush administration's aggressive effort to expand executive power in the fight against terrorism. But he also showed a willingness to stand up to the White House at critical times - most famously in a March 2004 visit to his hospital room over the wiretapping program.  Mr. Ashcroft was recovering from surgery when two top White House aides came to his hospital bedside and tried to persuade him to sign an extension for eavesdropping on Americans without warrants. He refused to certify its legality.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/ashcroft_john_per"/>
