  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/86566593942418775653">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/joel_i_klein/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-06</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Klein, Joel I</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">174</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 6, 2009
swept into Brooklyn on one of his first school visits as education secretary, calling him "someone I've learned a tremendous amount from." Arne Duncan In some circles, Joel I. Klein, New York's chancellor of public schools, is revered: as a star prosecutor turned crusader for the underclass, a fearless innovator willing to take on the powerful teachers' union. Australia's education minister flew him in for six days in November 2008, and
But among some of the state lawmakers who will determine the fate of the nation's largest school system, Mr. Klein is reviled: as an arrogant outsider obsessed with accountability, a tone-deaf suit unwilling to consider parents' views in what one politician called "a silencing of the lambs." After distancing himself from city and state lawmakers for more than six years, Mr. Klein has embarked on something of a charm offensive, phoning some Albany lawmakers to suggest meeting one on one.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.joel_klein"/>
