  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/83681979606911038793">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/u/mitsuko_uchida/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mitsuko Uchida is one of the most gifted and beloved of a strong middle generation of pianists that also includes Garrick Ohlsson, who won the Chopin Competition in Warsaw in 1970 (Ms. Uchida finished second), and Emanuel Ax (who finished seventh).
Ms. Uchida was born in Atami, Japan, near Tokyo, in 1948, and received her formative training in Vienna. She established her reputation in the 1980s playing and recording all the Mozart piano concertos and sonatas in London, where she has since lived, as well as in Tokyo.
Through further performances and recordings she has become an acknowledged master in other Viennese Classical and Romantic repertory, notably the concertos and sonatas of Beethoven and the sonatas of Schubert. Less widely known is her extensive and distinguished work in later repertories, ranging from the Second Viennese School (Schoenberg, Berg and Webern) to contemporary composers like Pierre Boulez and Gyorgy Kurtag.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.mitsuko_uchida"/>
