  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N88918981868152466523">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 19, 2009
Theodore B. Olson was little known outside Washington until the Republican Party called upon him to rescue George W. Bush's hopes of becoming president by arguing before the Supreme Court that ballot-recounting in Florida should stop.
Mr. Olson, who appeared twice before the justices, secured the 5-to-4 ruling on Dec. 12 that ended the recount and put Mr. Bush in the White House. He was rewarded with an appointment as Solicitor General, the Justice Department official who argues cases on behalf of the administration before the Supreme Court.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-02-28</nyt:first_use>
