    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BMcCain%2C+Cindy%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In 2000, Cindy McCain was mostly a cheerful sidekick to her husband on the presidential campaign trail and she kept her positions on national policy to herself.
When her husband made a second bid for the presidency in 2008, Mrs. McCain appeared a different kind of spouse. With two sons in the military, including one who has served in Iraq, she was far from the demure campaign wife.
She has had a role in her husband’s elections since 1982, when he first ran for Congress in her home state of Arizona and they worked the district together one door at a time.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.cindy_hensley_mccain"/>
  </rdf:Description>
