  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/27955610612287372383">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Kim Jong Il</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.kim_jong-il"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Aug. 24, 2009
Since North Korea was founded in 1948 under Soviet guardianship, it has had only two leaders: Kim Il-sung, and after his death in 1994, his son, Kim Jong-il, the first and only hereditary leader in the Communist world.
Under the younger Kim, North Korea has become a nuclear power. It has also become the world's most isolated state, one in which unknown numbers starved during famines in the 1990s, while money flowed to the country's military programs.
Although it was not until 1994 that Kim Jong-il became the official ruler of North Korea, he was all but running the country for years before that.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-27</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:number_of_variants>
