  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N20187293049711042473">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/manmohan_singh/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 18, 2009
Manmohan Singh is India's 13th prime minister and the first Sikh to win that position. He handily won a second term in May 2009.
A soft-spoken economist and former finance minister, Mr. Singh is credited with saving India from a balance-of-payments crisis and potential economic collapse in 1991, and with beginning many of the economic changes that have helped transform India's quasi-socialist economy into a growing global force.
The prime minister's signal accomplishment was a landmark nuclear agreement with the United States that ended 34 years of isolation for India among the world's nuclear power. The deal faced strong opposition at home, where parties -- including the Communists, Mr. Singh's allies in Parliament. But Mr. Singh survived a no-confidence vote, and the pact went on to win approval in the United States Senate as well.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/singh_manmohan_per"/>
