  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N72972577105371996703">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-25</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Zambian President Levy Mwanawasa, a favorite of Western donors and strong critic of Zimbabwean leader Robert Mugabe, died in a French hospital on Aug. 19, 2008, nearly two months after suffering a stroke.
Mr. Mwanawasa won praise abroad for tackling corruption and turning the copper-rich southern African country into one of the continent's biggest success stories, but opponents said he had failed to help most Zambians to escape poverty.
He became vice-president in 1991 and stunned observers after winning the presidency in 2001 by turning on former boss Frederick Chiluba.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/mwanawasa_levy_per"/>
