  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/23991309278678055083">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/m/riccardo_muti/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Italian conductor Riccardo Muti, who will become music director of the Chicago Symphony Orchestra in 2010, was born in 1941, the oldest of the five sons of a music-loving physician.
He has led some of the world's greatest orchestras. Perhaps most famously he was music director and conductor of the legendary La Scala opera house in Milan (1986-2005). Mr. Muti resigned from La Scala after the orchestra, the Filarmonica Della Scala, and hundreds of workers in the house interpreted his management decisions as threats to their job security.</skos:definition>
