    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Blahnik, Manolo</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">For nearly four decades, Manolo Blahnik, the high priest of the high heel, has made inimitable womenâ€™s footwear. His shoe designs were described by the Council of Fashion Designers of America, when he won its Stiletto Award in 1998, as â€˜â€˜collectibles for women for whom Barbies are too girlish and Ferraris not girlish enough.''
The elegant Mr. Blahnikâ€™s essential vision is of a shoe as high as a tower, with a heel as thin as a toothpick. These are the shoes that have become footwear icons, known to fashionistas as simply Manolos â€” shoes that some people call ''limousine shoes,'' on the assumption that you can wear them only if you never actually have to walk anywhere.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">9</nyt:associated_article_count>
