    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.eli_manning"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Aug. 5, 2009
Eli Manning is the quarterback of the New York Giants.
His  father, Archie, was the singular quarterback for the subpar New Orleans Saints, a role that rolled into further fame with a career as a public speaker. One of his older brothers, Peyton, was a star quarterback from the time Eli was in high school, and eventually led the Indianapolis Colts to a Super Bowl title in 2007, winning most valuable player honors for the game.
Eli Manning became quarterback at the University of Mississipi, and showed enough promise for the Giants to acquire him from San Diego in a blockbuster trade in 2004. For most of his time with the Giants, that deal had drawn mixed reviews. And as he struggled with consistency during the 2007-2008 regular season, leading the league in interceptions, it seemed lopsided, at best.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BManning%2C+Eli%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
