  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/62067758236340239973">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/gorey_edward_per"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-04-05</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">1925 ? 2000
Edward Gorey was known for writing at least 90 books, illustrating 60 others, and designing sets for stage productions, including "Dracula" on Broadway and "Amphigorey," an Off Broadway play he wrote. He won a 1978 Tony award for costume design for ''Dracula." His best-known characters included the Gashlycrumb Tinies, one woebegone child for each letter of the alphabet, from Amy, who fell down the stairs, to Zillah, who drank too much gin. His work was influenced by Ionesco and Buster Keaton, Goya and Matisse.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-04-05</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/g/edward_gorey/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.edward_gorey"/>
