  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N4462102105909573713">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 16, 2009
Iftikhar Muhammad Chaudhry became chief justice of Pakistan's Supreme Court in 2005 and soon became not only a central figure in the nation's political struggles but an icon to the country's legal profession and others campaigning for the rule of law. He was removed from office in November 2007 when President Pervez Musharraf declared a state of emergency, and reinstated on March 16th, 2009, as Mr. Musharraf's successor, Azif Ali Zardari, moved to avert a political crisis.
Mr. Chaudhry was born to a lower middle class family in the western city of Quetta in 1948, studied law at the local university and in 1974 started a practice there in various fields of law. In 1990, he became a judge of the Balochistan high court and was appointed to the Supreme Court in 2000, the year after General Musharraf seized power in a coup.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-07-03</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
