  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/47020002042752366693">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-24</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 28, 2009
in 2005 at the age of 51. She was also the first eastern German to lead the country after its reunification following the fall of the Berlin Wall. She is the leader of the Christian Democratic Union, a Catholic-leaning conservative party. Germany Angela Merkel became the first female chancellor of
Mrs. Merkel was re-elected on Sept. 27, 2009, strengthening her hand at home and abroad as one of the longest-serving leaders among Europe's major powers. But Mrs. Merkel's victory could nonetheless leave her political standing weaker in the long run because her party had its worst results in 60 years. A month later, at least nine members of her coalition withheld their vote and what was seen as a warning and prelude of uncertain times ahead.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-05-29</nyt:first_use>
