  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N79357584911854024743">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">If John Patrick Shanley, 58, more resembles a craftsman ? the wizened, handsome contractor ? than a playwright, it only makes sense. He builds stuff, including ?Doubt,? a cultural artifact so sturdy that it not only became a Broadway achiever, winning a Pulitzer Prize and a Tony for best play (2005), but a film starring Meryl Streep and Philip Seymour Hoffman that is among the mentioned in the 2008 Oscar race. By the way, he already has one of those for writing the 1987 film ?Moonstruck.?
A former marine and the son of an Irish immigrant father from County Westmeath, he has a lot of other prosaic credits in his rÃ©sumÃ©, including working as a locksmith, a bartender and an elevator operator. Born in the Bronx Oct. 30, 1950, he is very much a product of the kind of hard-knocks Roman Catholic primary school that frames the moral dilemma of ?Doubt.?</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/john_patrick_shanley/index.html"/>
