  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/22342768665500262123">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Feb. 9, 2009
Robert A. Lutz, the blunt-talking vice chairman of General Motors who is known as the auto industry's consummate "car guy," will retire at the end of 2009, G.M. announced Feb. 9, 2009.
Mr. Lutz, 76, who oversees product development at G.M., will become a senior adviser on April 1 before leaving the automaker in December. Thomas G. Stephens, currently executive vice president for engines and and quality, will succeed Mr. Lutz. Mr. Lutz, a former Marine fighter pilot whose passion for jets and muscle cars earned him the nickname "Maximum Bob," will leave G.M. at a critical time for the company, which must make a persuasive case to secure the last $4 billion loan of $13.4 billion aid package from Washington to avoid bankruptcy.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-07</nyt:first_use>
