  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/12332023334909729603">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mr. Nabokov, arguably the greatest prose stylist since Joyce, was born in Russia and settled in the United States in 1939, living here until 1959. He was best known as the author of "Lolita," a dazzling satirical novel about an aging professor's obsession with a 12 year old girl. The notoriety of that book in the popular imagination overshadowed the lasting legacy of his novels and short stories: exquisite, lapidary prose; a love of games and puzzles and coincidence; and self-conscious, self-reflexive narratives that would inform the work of several generations of writers to come, from John Updike and Martin Amis to John Barth and John Fowles.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.vladimir_nabokov"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
