  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N2811642238332389253">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-07</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jackson, Phil</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.phil_jackson"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 15, 2009
Phil Jackson has won more National Basketball Association championships than any coach in league history. His 10 N.B.A. titles include six with the Chicago Bulls and four with his current team, the Los Angeles Lakers. After winning the 2008-9 championship series in five games over the Orlando Magic, Jackson surpassed Red Auerbach, who won nine N.B.A. titles from 1950 to 1966, with the Boston Celtics.
Jackson has coached some of the greatest players in the league including Michael Jordan and Scottie Pippen in Chicago, and Shaquille O'Neal and Kobe Bryant in Los Angeles. In both cities he inherited the superstars who were accustomed to being the teams' primary offensive threat. But Jackson convinced them that a well-rounded team that included contributions from other players would make them even better.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jackson_phil_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-06-16</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
