  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/49823932861687875453">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-04-12</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The cult status of James J. Cramer, the host of the CNBC television show “Mad Money,” may derive even more from the manic atmosphere he creates on the show than the sagacity of his stock tips.
Mr. Cramer, the money manager-turned-media star, writes a flurry of Web columns on TheStreet.com and is the host of a daily radio show.
For a non-acolyte, the cult is tough to figure out. "Mad Money" is essentially televised talk radio, replete with a screaming, self-involved host; cheesy sound effects; and a call-in format that is not exactly visually engaging. Still, it is the top-rated program on CNBC.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
