  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/84239098587250908773">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/lazio_rick_a_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Lazio, Rick A</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/l/rick_a_lazio/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">in her first Senate race, is exploring the possibility of running for governor in 2010. Hillary Rodham Clinton , the Long Island Republican who ran against  Rick Lazio
Mr. Lazio, a House member who retired from Congress after losing to Mrs. Clinton in 2000, has had conversations with Republican elected officials, party leaders and fund-raisers to discuss the pros and cons of a possible candidacy, three people he has consulted about the race reported in January 2009.
At the time, it looked as if Mr. Lazio could have the Republican field to himself, despite the fact that he has been out of politics for nearly a decade. Many Republicans said the dearth of potential candidates for governor underscores the party’s weakness heading into the 2010 elections.</skos:definition>
