    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-23</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">For more than 25 years, Carl C. Icahn has taken positions in companies, large and small, and proceeded to make himself a burr in the side of management. In the process, he has also made himself one of the wealthiest men in the country, with a net worth estimated by Forbes at nearly $10 billion in 2007.
In 2006, his big fight was with Time Warner, a company whose stock had been stuck in neutral. After a prolonged battle of words ? during which Mr. Icahn advocated breaking the company up, and accused its chief executive, Richard D. Parsons, of being pretty much an empty suit ? Time Warner wound up increasing its stock buyback program and promising to cut costs. The settlement was widely viewed as a setback for Mr. Icahn. But the stock did rise, and Mr. Icahn?s then $5.2 billion hedge fund saw a $250 million gain.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-20</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BIcahn%2C+Carl+C%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
