  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N4298460726246369573">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-03-04</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/b/eli_broad/index.html"/>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BBroad%2C+Eli%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Broad, Eli</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">32</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In February 2008, reversing a previous decision to give his holdings to one or several museums, Mr. Broad said he would retain permanent control of his works in an independent foundation that makes loans to museums rather than give any of the art away. Long assumed to be at the top of the list of potential recipients of Mr. Broad's collection was the Los Angeles County Museum of Art, which in February 2008 opened the $56 million Broad Contemporary Art Museum, a building designed by Renzo Piano and financed by Mr. Broad.   Mr. Broad and his wife, Edythe, own a private collection of some 2,000 works of modern and contemporary art that is one of the most sought after by museums nationwide. He has long been a dominant force on the Los Angeles art scene, helping to found museums and serving on their boards, overseeing fund-raising for the construction of  Disney Hall, which was designed by Frank Gehry, and spurring a drive to revitalize downtown.  He has been especially active at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art, serving as a vice chairman of its board, leading fund-raising campaigns and strongly influencing the comings and goings of the museum's directors, including the hiring in 2006 of the current one, Michael Govan. The California billionaire financier and philanthropist Eli Broad made his fortune as the co-founder of Kaufman &amp; Broad, a builder of houses, and SunAmerica, a retirement-investment firm. Mr. Broad, born June 6, 1933,  enthusiastically engages himself in the public sphere, most visibly through his huge art collection and his philanthropic efforts in educational institutions across the country.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/broad_eli_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-12-24</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.eli_broad"/>
  </rdf:Description>
