  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/41235558623150337463">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">37</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Lewis, Kenneth D</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/l/kenneth_d_lewis/index.html"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/business.cik.1189892"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-07-01</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 15, 2009
Kenneth D. Lewis, who helped build Bank of America into one of the nation's largest banks, was stripped of his chairman's title on April 29, 2009. He remained chief executive, but the bank announced on Sept. 30 that he would step down by the end of the year.
Just two weeks later, on Oct. 15, 2009, Mr. Lewis announced that he would forgo his salary and bonus as chief executive this year. The announcement came as new legal questions emerged surrounding the troubled takeover of Merrill Lynch.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
