  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N85833741982379445753">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 8, 2009
Angelo R. Mozilo, a Bronx-born butcher's son, was chief executive of Countrywide Financial. He made a fortune shaking up the staid home mortgage business until the sub-prime housing crisis led to Bank of America absorbing the company in July 2008.
In June 2009, Mr. Mozilo was charged with securities fraud and insider trading in a civil suit brought by the Securities and Exchange Commission. Citing e-mail messages in which Mr. Mozilo referred to Countrywide loan products as "toxic" and "poison," S.E.C. officials said that he had misled investors about growing risks in the company's lending practices from 2005 through 2007. During this time, he also profited by selling stock in the company, gaining $140 million, the S.E.C. said.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-08-26</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Mozilo, Angelo R</skos:prefLabel>
