    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In 2008, in a state where Democrats are almost always considered vulnerable, Sen. Tim Johnson of South Dakota won re-election by 25 percentage points.
The National Journal's Almanac of American Politics 2008: Adapted from
Johnson was first elected to the Senate in 1996. He grew up in Canton, Flandreau and Vermillion in southeast South Dakota and went to the University of South Dakota, where he ultimately earned a law degree.
In the House, Johnson compiled a generally liberal voting record, though he voted for the balanced budget amendment. In 1996 Johnson ran against Republican Senator Larry Pressler and edged a narrow victory. In the Senate, Johnson’s voting record has been toward the center, though more liberal on foreign affairs issues. He supported the partial-birth abortion ban and cosponsored bills prohibiting meatpackers from owning, feeding or keeping livestock. He seldom got much publicity. “There are enough show horses in Washington to go around,” he said.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
