    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/rangel_charles_b_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 8, 2009
term, Charles B. Rangel represents the 15th Congressional District of New York, which includes Harlem and vast areas of northern Manhattan. A legend in his Harlem neighborhood and a shrewd player on Capitol Hill, Mr. Rangel became chairman of the Committee on Ways and Means, one of the most powerful bodies in American government, in January 2007. th First elected to the United States House of Representatives in 1970 and now serving his 20
Mr. Rangel had served on the committee, which produced the Social Security and Medicare laws, created the modern welfare state, and oversees the U.S. tax laws, since 1975. But with prestige and power came scrutiny, and it turned out that the blunt, gravelly-voiced Mr. Rangel, despite decades in Congress, had not received all that much of it.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
