    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 7, 2009
Alex Rodriguez is one of the top baseball players of his generation, and many have called him among the greatest of all time, since never before has a hitter combined such ability to hit for power and average with the Gold Glove talent in the infield. But he has never won the deep or lasting affection of fans, perhaps because his huge contract and a reputation for struggling in the postseason has obscured what he does during the regular season.
The Seattle Mariners selected Rodriguez with the first overall pick in the 1993 draft, and less than a year later, at 18, he played his first game in the majors. He won a batting title two years later, hitting .358. His power numbers also increased dramatically. In early 2001, he signed a 10-year contract with the Texas Rangers worth $250 million, the largest in sports history. The contract led to increased scrutiny and greater expectations. The demands escalated in early 2004, when the Rangers traded Rodriguez to the Yankees. He agreed to move to third base from shortstop, where he had won two Gold Gloves, making him and Derek Jeter partners on the left side of the infield.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/rodriguez_alex_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rodriguez, Alex</skos:prefLabel>
