  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N85128287073934402493">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.derek_walcott"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-05-31</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-31</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 4, 2009
The West Indian poet Derek Walcott won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1992 for writings that luminously evoked the cultural diversity and richness of the Caribbean.
Although acclaimed as a poet, Mr. Walcott is also a playwright, journalist and painter. He has often been praised for his ability to fuse the classics, folklore and history and to combine the vernacular and the grand manner.
One of Mr. Walcott's finest poetic work, "Omeros" (1990) runs 325 pages. Its principal characters have names taken from the "Iliad" and "Odyssey" of Homer. The poem was meant to capture "the whole experience of the people of the Caribbean," Mr. Walcott said.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
