    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">13</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Nov. 3, 2009
Vaclav Klaus, president of the Czech Republic and an iconoclast with a perfectly clipped mustache, provokes strong reactions.
To supporters, Mr. Klaus is a brave, lone crusader, a defender of liberty, the only European leader in the mold of the formidable Margaret Thatcher.
To his many critics, he is a cynical populist, a hardheaded pragmatist long known as a foil to former President Vaclav Havel, the philosopher-dreamer, and a troublemaker.
Before the Czech Republic assumed the six month rotating presidency of the European Union in January 2009, Mr. Klaus criticized the course set by the union's preceding leader, President Nicolas Sarkozy of France. The ambitious Mr. Sarkozy used France's European Union presidency to push an agenda that included broader and more coordinated regulation by the largest economies to tame the worst of the market's excesses.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/vaclav_klaus/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.vaclav_klaus"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-11</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Klaus, Vaclav</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
