    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.martin_margiela"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Martin Margiela, shown above in a 1997 photo, is a Belgian designer closely associated with the deconstructionist movement that dominated fashion during the mid-1990s, a look favoring raw edges, abstract shapes and minimal embellishment. In his work he often recycles nonfashion materials (plastic, bottle caps, hangers) into runway designs, or "recreates" vintage store finds to make new pieces that look identical to the old ones.
Mr. Margiela's runway presentations are often fraught with metaphors about contemporary notions of dressing and sexuality, although deciphering his messages is typically a challenging endeavor. Throughout his career, the designer has refused to be photographed or to grant traditional interviews, instead choosing to respond to questions with cryptic notes by fax, signed "Maison Martin Margiela" (implying he is not entirely responsible for their content). His clothing is also unsigned, bearing only a white rectangular label printed with a series of numbers, one of them circled to correspond to a particular division, like women's (1), men's (10) or shoes (22).</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/m/martin_margiela/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/margiela_martin_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Margiela, Martin</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">15</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-09</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
