    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">and symphony orchestra in St. Petersburg, which tour under the name Kirov. opera The conductor Valery Gergiev has become one of Russia’s most potent cultural symbols. Like few other musicians, he wields extraordinary power in his country as head of Russia’s musical crown jewel, the Maryinsky Theater and its ballet,
, the Russian prime minister and former president. The two men are friendly but not friends, he said, and know each other from Mr. Putin’s days in the early 1990s as a St. Petersburg deputy mayor. Mr. Gergiev has long been a strong and vocal supporter of Mr. Putin’s leadership. Vladimir V. Putin , and praised Russia as a savior. His willingness to make Russia’s case again raised questions about his relationship with  South Ossetia In August 2008, Mr. Gergiev denounced Georgia’s bombing of his homeland, the breakaway region of</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/gergiev_valery_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-02</nyt:latest_use>
