    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/ma_ying_jeou_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.ma_ying-jeou"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Update: July 27, 2009
Ma Ying-jeou is the president of Taiwan. On July 26, he won reelection as chief of the country's governing party. The victory could bolster Mr. Ma's efforts to pursue engagement and economic ties with China, analysts said.
Beijing officials have viewed Taiwan as a renegade province ever since the Nationalists retreated to the island upon losing China's civil war to the Communists in 1949. The reunification of mainland China and Taiwan has long been the mainland's top goal in relations with the United States, Taiwan's closest ally.  14 months ago as Taiwan's president, Mr. Ma has pursued warmer relations with China, but not always with the wholehearted support of his own party. his election Mr. Ma ran uncontested for the post of Kuomintang party chief, winning 92.5 percent of about 300,000 votes cast, with a turnout of 58 percent, the party said. Since</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-03-25</nyt:first_use>
