  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/11025492216186299103">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-10-28</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Livni, Tzipi</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Feb. 20, 2009
After rising through the Kadima Party, Tzipi Livni has acted as Israel's minister of foreign affairs since May 2006 and as the head of Israel's negotiating team with the Palestinians. Ms. Livni, who favors a two-state solution to the Israeli-Palestinian problem, replaced Prime Minister Ehud Olmert as head of the party in internal elections on Sept. 18, 2008. Mr. Olmert tendered his resignation three days after the vote.
’s far-right Yisrael Beiteinu Party, out of 120 seats in Parliament. But the total gain of all parties on the right far outweighed those of the left, and on Feb. 20 Mr. Netanyahu was chosen to try to form a government as the new prime minister. Avigdor Lieberman ’s elections on Feb. 10, 2009, ended with Ms. Livni's center-right Kadima Party edging past the right-wing  Likud party of Benjamin Netanyahu 28 seats to 27, with 15 for   Israel</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.tzipi_livni"/>
