  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/49198045094198574693">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Plath, Sylvia</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-04-12</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 23, 2009
On a cold February day in 1963, with her two small children upstairs in their room, Sylvia Plath put her head inside her kitchen oven and turned on the gas. In the wake of her violent death, reams of material have been published, analyzing her life and work, the most famous being "The Bell Jar." She has been dismissed as a case study in female neurosis and Freudian guilt, held up as a victim of a male-dominated society, enshrined as a tragic heroine (''a Dido, Phaedra or Medea,'' in Robert Lowell's words), explicated as a symbol of contemporary angst.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
