    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated:  Sept. 25, 2009
Joe Girardi, a former catcher known for his sharp baseball mind and ability to work well with pitchers, is the manager of the New York Yankees.
A native of Washington, Ill., near Peoria, Girardi played baseball at Northwestern University while earning a degree in industrial engineering. He played in the major league for 15 years. What kept him on big-league rosters was his soundness as a catcher and his baseball mind -- his knack for putting together pitch sequences that defeated hitters. Playing for the Chicago Cubs, Colorado Rockies, St. Louis Cardinals and Yankees, he finished with a .267 career average, and hit just 36 home runs in more than 4,000 major-league at-bats.</skos:definition>
