  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/28176223110482099813">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 1, 2009
Lee Myung-bak, South Korea's president, owes much of his electoral victory to a wildly successful project he completed as the mayor of Seoul: the restoration in 2005 of a paved-over, four-mile stream in the city's downtown, over which an ugly highway had been built during the growth-at-all-cost 1970’s. The new stream became a Central Park-like gathering place, tapped into a growing national emphasis on quality of life and immediately made the Mr. mayor, Lee Myung-bak, a top presidential contender. The media’s glowing reviews, though, sometimes pointed out that the highway had been built by a construction company once headed by none other than Mr. Lee himself.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/lee_myung_bak_per"/>
