    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/r/m_jodi_rell/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Nov. 10, 2009
In Connecticut political circles, M. Jodi Rell was often referred to as the "accidental governor." It was not necessarily a pejorative term, since Mrs. Rell, a Republican, steadied the state after Gov. John G. Rowland resigned in 2004 amid a corruption scandal. In November 2009, Mrs. Rell announced that she would not seek re-election. She did not give a specific reason.
The surprising news left the Republicans without a candidate possessing big statewide name recognition and the Democrats with an opportunity to push for the governorship in 2010.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-01</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rell, M Jodi</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.m_jodi_rell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
