    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Golisano, Tom</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.tom_golisano"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/g/tom_golisano/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">15</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/golisano_tom_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated July 9, 2009
Tom Golisano is the owner of the Buffalo Sabres, and a three-time candidate for governor of New York. A billionaire, he is a major political and philanthropic force in western New York.
Term limits and campaign and governmental reform are at the center of Mr. Golisano's political efforts. In 2008, Mr. Golisano committed to spending $5 million to support candidates in state legislative races that he felt would work for a more open political culture in Albany.
But he felt betrayed that Senate Democrats didn't act more boldly when they took control in January. He felt especially slighted when he tried to reach out to the new Senate leader, Malcolm Smith, in March 2009. That failed meeting led to the dramatic collapse on June 8 of the Democrats' grip on the Senate majority as a frustrated Mr. Golisano secretly planned with Republicans to persuade two Democrats to join them in ousting Mr. Smith. He played a major role in brokering the deal in which the two Democratic Senators withdrew their support for the majority, effectively returning control of the Senate to the Republicans.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-08</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
