  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/75292914099668696323">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-14</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Sep. 2, 2009
John Paul Stevens, a soft-spoken Republican and former antitrust lawyer from Chicago, arrived at the Supreme Court in December 1975. He had been appointed by President Gerald Ford to succeed Justice William O. Douglas, who had retired the month before. On the court at that time were Thurgood Marshall and William J. Brennan Jr., who along with Justice Douglas had been liberal stalwarts of the Warren court era. Also serving were Lewis F. Powell Jr., a Nixon appointee who voted with the court's conservatives on criminal justice issues but was a strong supporter of abortion rights, and Potter Stewart, the last of President Dwight D. Eisenhower’s four Supreme Court appointees, like Justice Stevens a moderate Republican from the Midwest.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">25</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/john_paul_stevens/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.john_paul_stevens"/>
