    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/knight_bob_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-21</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Bob Knight, who won more games than any men's coach in major college basketball history, resigned from his position at Texas Tech on Feb. 5, 2008, in the middle of the 2007-8 season. Soon after, he became a high-profile sports analyst for ESPN.
On Jan. 10, 2008, Northern State men's basketball coach Don Meyer passed Bob Knight to become the NCAA's all-time leader with his 903rd victory. Knight notched his 900th victory on Jan. 16, 2008, against Texas A&amp;M. Knight spent seven seasons coaching at Texas Tech, and his son Pat took over the team as coach. Knight's years at Indiana were defined by teams that were perennially national powers, as well as by his own bombast. Knight went 662-239 as coach of the Hoosiers from 1971 through 2000. He began his career in 1965 at Army, where he coached the current Duke coach, Mike Krzyzewski. He ran programs that steered clear of N.C.A.A. violations and consistently graduated players, but he also clashed with university officials and was caught on videotape grabbing the neck of one of his players at Indiana. That precipitated his departure in 2000 for what the university termed a ''pattern of unacceptable behavior.'' He won a record 902 games over all and captured three national titles at Indiana University and is a member of the Basketball Hall of Fame. While his success made him one of the foremost coaches of his generation, Knight became just as well known for his temper and his defiance of authority. He left behind a legacy as one of the most successful and controversial coaches in history.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/bob_knight/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-02-18</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
  </rdf:Description>
