    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 5, 2009
Asif Ali Zardari was described by his wife, Benazir Bhutto, as the "Mandela of Pakistan." But while she was prime minister, Mr. Zardari earned another nickname: "Mr. 10 Percent," for his reputation of demanding kickbacks on government contracts. In September 2008, he became president of Pakistan after Ms. Bhutto's assassination propelled her party into control of Parliament.
His government has been challenged by increasing attacks by Taliban and Qaeda insurgencies in tribal areas and along the country's western border with Afghanistan. There has been increasing concern in the Obama administration about the survivability of the Zardari government.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-23</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
  </rdf:Description>
