    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-07-14</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/snow_tony_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">June 1, 1955 — July 12, 2008
Tony Snow, a conservative columnist and television commentator who served for 17 months as President Bush’s press secretary, died after a long battle with colon cancer on July 12, 2008.
Mr. Snow became press secretary in April 2006, as part of a staff shake-up across the upper echelons of the White House, as the administration sought to mend its sagging political fortunes.
A longtime radio and television pundit and newspaper columnist, Mr. Snow in those roles had sometimes been critical of Mr. Bush, while broadly supportive of his conservative policies. As spokesman, he brought a far more easygoing style to press briefings than had been shown by either of his predecessors, Scott McClellan and Ari Fleisher, although the White House and the press corps continued to joust over Iraq war and other issues.</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Snow, Tony</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/tony_snow/index.html"/>
