  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/31584421951495729803">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-06</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">By the time Louis Armstrong died in 1971, he had profoundly changed the culture of the world he was born into. His affability and his musical excellence, underscored by comic genius, combined to make him one of the first great pop figures of the 20th century. He helped create the balancing act between the fine arts and entertainment that has defined the best products of modern American culture.
"Armstrong is the Prometheus of the blues idiom," the jazz scholar Albert Murray once observed, "and through him the United States exercised its impact on 20th-century esthetics." But after Armstrong's death, the stature of his work declined somewhat. He had spent much of the previous 20 years working in small groups, and critical opinion tended to overlook him. His synthesis of entertainment and music offended purists, while younger musicians were interested in the avant-garde or in be-bop.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
