    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">12</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-23</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-09-21</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.phil_rizzuto"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Phil Rizzuto 1917 - 2007
Phil Rizzuto - forever "The Scooter" - was the New York Yankee shortstop for 13 seasons in the 1940s, an undersized (5 feet, 6 inches and 150 pounds) sparkplug on a team known for its slugging. He was a terrific bunter and defensive specialist.
In 1950, Rizzuto was named the American League's most valuable player for a season in which he hit .324, made only 14 errors and turned 123 double plays.
Rizzuto was born Sept. 25, 1917, and grew up in Brooklyn and Queens. He tried out for the Giants and Dodgers (his future Yankee manager Casey Stengel, then the skipper of the Dodgers, told him to get a shoeshine box) but was deemed too small.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/r/phil_rizzuto/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rizzuto, Phil</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
