    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated March 30, 2009
Joseph R. Biden is the vice president of the United States. He served in the Senate from 1972, when he was elected at the age of 29, until taking office with President Obama on Jan. 20, 2009. Known primarily in the Senate for his knowledge of foreign policy, in the early days of the Obama administration he has been influential in shaping a new approach to Afghanistan. Mr. Obama also put Mr. Biden in charge of a task force on improving the situation of the middle class.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">6</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/b/joseph_r_jr_biden/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Biden, Joseph R Jr</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N47619127052849218403">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">27</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-07</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 22, 2009
Betsy Gotbaum, a Democrat, is the New York City public advocate, serving as ombudsman for complaints about city government. Under the City Charter, she is next in line to succeed the mayor.
Ms. Gotbaum was elected in 2001 to an ill-defined position that comes with a salary of $150,000 and a staff of 47. Ms. Gotbaum has used the post to bring attention to education, child welfare and women's issues, among others.
But her tenure has largely been characterized as low profile, and criticized by some political observers and rivals as not being aggressive and confrontational enough with the Bloomberg administration.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/g/betsy_gotbaum/index.html"/>
