    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Lloyd C. Blankfein has been the chief executive of Goldman, Sachs since June 2006, replacing Henry Paulson, who left to become treasury secretary in the Bush administration.
Presiding over one of the richest financial enterprises in history, Mr. Blankfein, who began as a gold salesman, is a far cry from a cinematic idea of a chief executive.
â€œLess than a decade ago, he could be seen in shorts at a golf outing, tube socks stretched to his knees, 50 pounds heavier, and toting his BlackBerry in the same plastic bag as his bagel with cream cheese,â€ wrote Jenny Anderson of The New York Times in a June 10, 2007, profile. â€œToday, he dons navy pinstripes and a power tie and, having just returned from a business trip to Turkey, enjoys conversing about the Ottoman Empire.â€</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
