    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Grass, Gunter</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-03-08</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 16, 2009
for Literature for "The Tin Drum" and other works in 1999. Mr. Grass was born on Oct. 16, 1927, in the Polish port of Gdansk, when the city was known as Danzig. Nobel Prize The German author Günter Grass won the
With his first novel, "The Tin Drum" (1959), the provocative and comically disturbing story of a young German who lived through the horrors of Nazi Germany and who willfully stunts his growth and bangs a toy drum in anarchistic protest, Mr. Grass established himself as a voice of conscience and controversy for postwar Germany. That reputation continued and grew with the publication of, among others works of fiction, nonfiction, poetry and illustrations, the novella "Cat and Mouse" (1963), and the novels "Dog Years" (1963), "From the Diary of a Snail" (1973) and "The Rat" (1987).</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">15</nyt:associated_article_count>
  </rdf:Description>
