  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/86936202902440421073">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/moktada_al_sadr/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.muqtada_al-sadr"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Sadr, Moktada Al-</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-06-27</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In the years that followed the 2003 invasion, Moktada al-Sadr became the most important political figure in Iraq. He combined an unwavering opposition to the American occupation, the loyalty of the Mahdi Army militia and a network that provided social services to the poorest of the countryâ€™s Shiite majority to brush aside older, more prestigious and better-established rivals, amassing power that often seemed to overshadow that of the government.
He also became one of the countryâ€™s most divisive figures. As the Mahdi Army gained power in the spiraling violence of 2006 it was linked to widespread killings, the ethnic cleansing of Baghad neighborhoods and gangsterism.</skos:definition>
