    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/maloney_carolyn_b_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">13</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/m/carolyn_b_maloney/index.html"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-23</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 7, 2009
, but she announced on Aug. 7, 2009, that she had decided against running for Senate. Kirsten E. Gillibrand Carolyn B. Maloney, a Manhattan congresswoman, was a potential 2010 challenger against Senator
Ms. Maloney, a quintessential New York liberal usually embraced by the party, had found herself very much alone -- in fact, something of an outcast, in her quest for the Senate.
Democratic leaders including President Obama had embarked on an intense effort to nudge potential challengers out of the race out of concerns that a bruising primary battle would have weakened Ms. Gillibrand against a Republican challenge in the general election.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
