  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/6727580816423805663">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/karzai_hamid_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Dec. 8, 2009
, the extreme Islamic fundamentalist militia that took power in the mid-1990s. A charming, urbane tribal leader who favors flowing capes, he was a White House favorite during most of the Bush administration, but these days he is not so favored, not by Washington and not by his fellow Afghans. Taliban Hamid Karzai is the president of Afghanistan. He has led his country for the more than seven years since the fall of the
, refused to take part in a runoff vote, accusing the government of profound corruption and electoral fraud. Abdullah Abdullah Mr. Karzai was handed another five-year term in 2009 after his main rival in the presidential election,</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">288</nyt:associated_article_count>
