  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/91507436553969479723">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Alan R. Mulally was named chief executive and president of the Ford Motor Company in September 2006. He had been a top executive of Boeing, where he had worked for 37 years, and had no previous experience in the auto industry.
William Clay Ford Jr., great-grandson of the founder of the Ford Motor Company, brought in Mr. Mulally in a tacit acknowledgment that he needed a new approach in his efforts to turn around the struggling automaker. Mr. Ford said he planned to give Mr. Mulally responsibility for solving Ford’s many problems, including falling sales, slumping profits and the loss of market share to its Asian rivals.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-09-06</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
