    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/w/serena_williams/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Nov. 30, 2009
Serena Williams, an American tennis star known for her confident athleticism, aggressive style and thundering strokes from the baseline, has been ranked No. 1 in the world on four separate occasions.
In July 2009 she was the defending champion of the U.S. Open, Australian Open and Wimbledon singles tournaments, and was ranked No. 2 in the world. Â 
In a 2009 U.S. Open semi-final match, Williams lost to eventual champion Kim Clijsters and shocked fans with a profanity-laced outburst at a line judge that cost her the final point of a 6-4, 7-5 defeat. Williams was fined a recordÂ $175,000 and put on probation, meaning that she could be suspended from the U.S. Open if she has another ''major offense'' at any Grand Slam in the next two years. The fine will be reduced to $82,500 if she commits no further major offence through 2011.</skos:definition>
