  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N63135843457566534063">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Vikram S. Pandit was named Citigroup's chief executive officer in December 2007, bringing his background as an investment banker with a Ph.D. in finance to the vexing problems facing the giant global bank and the global economy.
Mr. Pandit joined Citigroup in July 2007, with a mandate to run its alternative investments unit and bolster a management roster that lacked a clear successor to Charles O. Prince, its chairman and chief executive.
His rise was meteoric. Just weeks into his job running the alternative investment unit, Mr. Pandit was tapped by Mr. Prince to figure out ways to stave off billions of dollars in losses from bad investments related to subprime mortgages. By mid-October, Mr. Prince broadened his responsibilities to include oversight of the investment bank. And after Mr. Prince abruptly resigned in November after announcing $18 billion in write-downs, Mr. Pandit succeeded him as chief executive.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.vikram_pandit"/>
