    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BLuPone%2C+Patti%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.patti_lupone"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">LuPone, Patti</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated April 20,2009
Patti Lupone won the Tony Award as best actress in a musical in 2008 for her performance as Momma Rose in a Broadway revival of "Gypsy." She is a theatrical powerhouse, a renowned entertainer, a diva with a (deserved or not) reputation for being difficult, a "musical cult goddess" (as Ben Brantley of The Times has called her).
And yet her career has not been untroubled. People haven't generally written roles for her, as they surely would have in an earlier Broadway era -- the defining problem in a career that doesn't so much resemble a path as a Rorschach blot, read it how you wish.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
