    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 25, 2009
By Andrew L. Yarrow
John Hope Franklin was a prolific scholar of African-American history who profoundly influenced thinking about slavery and Reconstruction. He died March 25, 2009, in Durham, N.C. He was 94.
During a career of scholarship, teaching and advocacy that spanned more than six decades, Dr. Franklin was deeply involved in the painful debates that helped reshape America's racial identity, working with the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., W.E.B. Du Bois, Thurgood Marshall and other giants of the 20th century.</skos:definition>
