    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/alfonsin_raul_per"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.raul_alfonsin"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">'s president from 1983 to 1989. His presidency symbolized the return of democracy in Argentina and other Latin American nations after an era of military dictatorships. He died on March 31, 2009. Argentina Raúl Alfonsín served as
Mr. Alfonsín's time in office was one of upheaval that included three failed military coup attempts, hyperinflation and food riots. He won wide praise for prosecuting the military dictators who had preceded him in office.
Raúl Ricardo Alfonsín Foulkes was born to a family of shopkeepers in Chascomús on March 12, 1927. His father, an immigrant from Spain, was a passionate supporter of the Loyalists in the Spanish Civil War and a foe of Franco. He graduated from a military academy with a bachelor's degree and the rank of second lieutenant, but he said he "became fed up with the military."</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Alfonsin, Raul</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
