  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/88493590100698203013">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/t/gay_talese/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.gay_talese"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/talese_gay_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Talese, Gay</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-10</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-29</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated April 16, 2009
Gay Talese is the author of the best-selling books "Honor Thy Father," his 1971 study of the Bonanno crime family, and "Thy Neighbor's Wife," his 1980 report about the sexual revolution. He is also a former reporter for The New York Times, and wrote the classic 1969 institutional portrait of The Times, "The Kingdom and the Power."
The writer and former Times reporter David Halberstam once called him "the most important nonfiction writer of his generation, the person whose work most influenced at least two generations of other reporters."</skos:definition>
