    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">23</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-09-28</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: July 31, 2009
Spanish Prime Minister José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero, 47, seized the narrow mandate he won from an electorate angered about the way Spain's conservative government handled the terrorist bombings of March 11, 2004 to propel a country once gripped by religious conservatism into the liberal vanguard of Europe.
One of his first acts as prime minister was to withdraw Spanish troops from Iraq. He has pushed to legalize gay marriage and adoption by same-sex couples, reduce the role of religion in schools, grant more political power to Spain's provinces and better integrate immigrants.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/zapatero_jose_luis_rodriguez_per"/>
