    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-31</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 19, 2009
, appeared to have neutralized for now the reform forces that they saw as a threat to their power, political analysts said. Mahmoud Ahmadinejad Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, Iran's supreme leader since 1989, is the country's true authority, with final say over all matters of state and faith. With the June 2009 presidential election, Ayatollah Khamenei and his protégé, President
But with more than half a million protesters massed in the streets of Tehran to challenge the outcome of the vote, and demonstrations reported in other cities, Ayatollah Khamenei has called for a high-level inquiry into accusations of election irregularities. By the end of the week, the ayatollah had taken an unequivocal stand against days of mass protests. Ayatollah Khamenei sternly warned opposition supporters on June 19 to stay off the streets and raised the prospect of violence if their defiant, vast demonstrations continued.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
