  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/57964178437290573413">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">61</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-12-03</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jones_marion_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/j/marion_jones/index.html"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jones, Marion</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.marion_jones"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-28</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Nov. 30, 2009
On Jan. 11, 2008, Olympic gold medal winner Marion Jones was sentenced to six months in prison and ordered to perform 800 hours of community service after she pleaded guilty in October to lying to federal agents about her use of performance-enhancing drugs and her connection to a check-fraud case.
The sentencing marks a tragic end to what began as a promising athletic career for the talented Ms. Jones, who won three gold medals and two bronze medals in the Sydney 2000 Olympics, becoming the first female athlete to win five medals in track and field in one Olympics. But even in 2000, she was already becoming embroiled in a steroids scandal that put those achievements in question.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
