  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/84765217601391713893">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Thain, John A</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/t/john_a_thain/index.html"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.john_thain"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-05-01</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Even for Wall Street's Mr. Fix-It, the credit crisis was too huge.
In November 2007, John A. Thain was tapped to revitalize the battered Merrill Lynch. The investment bank had been hit hard by the subprime mortgage crisis, and Mr. Thain, a former president of Goldman Sachs, had won plaudits earlier for turning around the embattled New York Stock Exchange.
With Mr. Thain selling the idea of a fresh start to investors, more than $12 billion was raised within months. But company losses, and the write-downs, were mounting. In April 2008, Merrill announced a nearly $2 billion loss for its first quarter and said it intended to lay off more than 2,900 employees.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">22</nyt:associated_article_count>
