    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.kareem_abdul-jabbar"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-01-02</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Abdul-Jabbar, Kareem</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/a/kareem_abduljabbar/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Nov. 10, 2009
Kareem Abdul-Jabbar holds the N.B.A.'s all-time scoring record, of 38,387 points. During his 20-year career he redefined the role of center, won six Most Valuable Player awards and was on six teams that won the N.B.A. championship.
On Nov. 10, 2009, Abdul-Jabbar announced that he has leukemia. The intensely private athlete-turned-writer said he was making the disclosure to help dispel myths about the disease.
Abdul-Jabbar said that he learned in December 2008 that he has chronic myeloid leukemia, a cancer of the blood and bone marrow in which the body produces cancerous white blood cells. Chronic describes a relatively slow-growing cancer that may take years to progress. Myeloid refers to the type of white blood cell being overproduced.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-10</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
