    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Whiting, Margaret</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.margaret_whiting"/>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BWhiting%2C+Margaret%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated April 9, 2009
Margaret Whiting is a singer famous for hits like "That Old Black Magic" and "Moonlight in Vermont."
She was born in 1924. Her father, Richard Whiting, was a pre-eminent contributor to those golden decades of pop song, the 1920s and 1930s, with hits like "Hooray for Hollywood," "Sleepytime Gal" and "Beyond the Blue Horizon." In the world of pop song, Ms. Whiting grew up as a princess. The Whiting house in Hollywood was constantly filled with such colleagues of her father as Harold Arlen, Johnny Mercer, Gus Kahn and Jerome Kern, who was ''Uncle Jerry.'' </skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-17</nyt:latest_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/whiting_margaret_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/w/margaret_whiting/index.html"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
  </rdf:Description>
