    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">7</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/h/sally_hemings/index.html"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Sally Hemings (1773-1835), was a slave at Thomas Jefferson’s Monticello, pictured, and was the third president’s mistress, according to a DNA analysis in 1998.
Hemings was the daughter of Elizabeth (Betty) Hemings and, allegedly, John Wayles, Thomas Jefferson’s father-in-law. She became Thomas Jefferson’s property as part of his inheritance from the Wayles estate in 1774 and came with her mother to Monticello by 1776.
Though it was rumored in Jefferson’s time that he had a sexual relationship with “dashing Sally,” many historians largely dismissed the claim for two centuries, and for most of that time American society would have had a hard time accepting that a founding father with a character as lofty as Jefferson’s would have such a liaison. Hemings was only 14 when she accompanied Jefferson’s daughter to Paris in 1787.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.sally_hemings"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-10-05</nyt:latest_use>
