  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/82357249205336154973">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-05</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.jane_jacobs"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">10</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jacobs_jane_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jacobs, Jane</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated August 5, 2009
By Douglas Martin
Jane Jacobs, the hugely influential writer and social critic, died April 25, 2006, at age 89.
In her book "The Death and Life of Great American Cities," written in 1961, Ms. Jacobs transcended her own withering critique of 20th-century urban planning and proposed radically new principles for rebuilding cities: 1. A street or district must serve several primary functions. 2. Blocks must be short. 3. Buildings must vary in age, condition and use. 4. Population must be dense.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/j/jane_jacobs/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-04-26</nyt:first_use>
