    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-07-10</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Johnson, Lady Bird</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Lady Bird Johnson 1912-2007
Lady Bird Johnson, the widow of President Lyndon B. Johnson, who was once described by her husband as "the brains and money of this family" and whose business skills cushioned his road to the White House, died July 11, 2007 at her home in Austin, Tex. She was 94.
Mrs. Johnson was a calm and steadying influence on her often moody and volatile husband as she quietly attended to the demands imposed by his career. Liz Carpenter, her press secretary during her years in the White House, once wrote that "if President Johnson was the long arm, Lady Bird Johnson's was the gentle hand."</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/johnson_lady_bird_per"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/j/lady_bird_johnson/index.html"/>
