  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/47199961499895701093">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/user.jamie.nytdataid.47199961499895701093"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">6</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/thomas_krens/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A towering 6 foot 5, with an M.B.A. in management from Yale and a manner that is often taken for arrogance, Thomas Krens, 61, was long synonymous with the Guggenheim Museum. Over the course of his nearly 20-year tenure as the director of the Solomon R. Guggenheim Foundation, he was best known for his ambitions for developing an international network from Las Vegas to Bilbao, Spain, and for high-profile exhibitions from “The Art of the Motorcycle” to ones that tackled entire countries like China and Brazil. He stepped down from his position at the Guggenheim on February 27, but will not be leaving the museum altogether. In a statement the foundation emphasized that Mr. Krens would remain at the foundation as a senior adviser for international affairs, overseeing the creation of a 452,000-square-foot museum in Abu Dhabi to be designed by Frank Gehry.</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Krens, Thomas</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
