    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.max_cleland"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-07-19</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cleland, Max</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Feb. 12, 2009
Max Cleland has served as a senator from Georgia (1997-2003), Georgia secretary of state (1982-1996) and administrator of the United States Veterans Administration, the predecessor to the Department of Veterans Affairs (1977-1981).
He has a strong military background as a former Army captain in Vietnam, where he was gravely wounded and became a triple amputee. He gained federal experience in veterans affairs under President Jimmy Carter. He now lobbies for a health care company specializing in tissue regeneration technology.
Some people, particularly liberals, view him as a hero for his vocal condemnation of President Bush after an onslaught of negative Republican advertising helped cost him re-election to the Senate against Saxby Chambliss in 2002. But Mr. Cleland has also been criticized as accomplishing little in the Senate and for supporting Mr. Bush’s 2001 tax cuts.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-07-31</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
