  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/46209400997597176533">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-09-21</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: May 21, 2009
Barry Scheck has made a career of getting innocent men released from prison. He is the co-founder of the Innocence Project, a nonprofit legal clinic at Yeshiva University's Benjamin M. Cardozo School of Law in New York. He and co-founder Peter J. Neufeld pursue cases in which DNA testing might exonerate a prisoner.
Since the founding of the Innocence Project in 1992, more than 215 people in the country have been exonerated. At one time, 16 of those individuals had been sentenced to death. The project has helped free inmates by showing that DNA samples taken at the scene of a crime were different from those taken from the defendants.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/barry_scheck/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.barry_scheck"/>
