    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Delay, Tom</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BDelay%2C+Tom%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Aug. 18, 2009
Tom DeLay, the fiery former House majority leader, resigned Congress in 2006 after his indictment in Texas on charges of violating campaign finance laws. His resignation came amid accusations that he had funneled $190,000 in corporate contributions to Republicans in the state's 2002 legislative elections. The case has not yet gone to trial.
, which also serves as a fund-raising vehicle. tomdelay.com Shortly after leaving Congress, Mr. DeLay, nicknamed the Hammer by colleagues for his tough-minded legislative tactics, began a comeback of sorts. The centerpiece for Mr. DeLay's return to the capital has been his acerbic Web site,</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.tom_delay"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
