  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N83555554132685526473">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.mohammed_zahir_shah"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Zahir Shah, Mohammed</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mohammad Zahir Shah, 1914-2007
For the last five years of his life Mohammad Zahir Shah served as a fragile symbol of unity for the country he ruled for four decades. He ascended to the throne of Afghanistan in 1933 at the age of 19 after his father was assassinated before his eyes. For most of his time as king, he was an inactive ruler, leaving the exercise of power to his uncles while tending to his farms. In the 1960's, however, he introduced a series of reform, including a constitutional monarchy and rights for women in voting, schools and the workforce. The moves were not popular with Islamic traditionalists, and in 1973 he was ousted by a cousin while traveling in Italy for eye surgery. He abdicated his throne rather than provoke bloodshed, and stayed in Italy.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
