    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.steve_fossett"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">26</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Fossett, Steve</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated July 10, 2009
Steve Fossett 1944 - 2008
Steve Fossett, the millionaire adventurer who disappeared Sept. 3, 2007 after taking off from a ranch for a brief recreational flight in a single-engine plane was declared dead on Feb. 15, 2008. He was 63.
The wreckage of that plane was discovered on Oct. 1, 2008 in the east-central mountains of California. Mr. Fossett died when his aircraft ran into a mountain downdraft, the National Transportation Safety Board reported in July 2009.
Mr. Fossett was a veteran aviator known for his quests to set world records. In 2002, on his sixth attempt, he became the first person to fly around the world uninterrupted in a hot air balloon. Last year, he completed the longest nonstop flight in aviation history, flying 26,389 miles in 76 hours in a lightweight experimental plane. He also set and chased records on land and sea, including the round-the-world speed record for sailboats.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/f/steve_fossett/index.html"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
