  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N81144576928328558413">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.willie_randolph"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Randolph, Willie</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Willie Randolph was appointed to the post of Mets manager in November 2004. At the time, Lee Jenkins of The New York Times described the move as a "fairy tale come full circle.'' Randolph grew up in Brooklyn rooting for the Mets, became an all-start second baseman for the Yankees, where he was a pillar of quiet in a notably turbulent era, played his last major league game as a Met and then served 10 years as a Yankee coach.
In September 2007, that fairy tale became something of a baseball nightmare, as the Mets blew a seven-game lead for one of the greatest late-season collapses of all time. When the team stumbled again in May 2008 speculation rose that Randolph would be out of a job unless the team's fortunes changed soon. The normally unflappable Randolph lashed out, criticizing what he said was a racial double-standard. He quickly apologized for the remark, calling it a distraction, and the team's management gave him a Memorial Day vote of confidence. But as the team continued to struggle, Randolph was relieved of his duties on June 17, 2008.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/r/willie_randolph/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/randolph_willie_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">160</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-30</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
