  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/88894774422009985253">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">10</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The day before the inauguration, Mr. Warren gave the keynote address at the annual birthday service for the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. in Atlanta, drawing about 100 protesters with signs reading “No bigotry in MLK’s church” and “We still have a dream.” In December 2008, Barack Obama chose Mr. Warren to deliver the invocation at his inauguration, making the pastor a lightning rod for controversy because of his outspoken opposition to abortion and same-sex marriage. Although the choice was meant as an olive branch to conservative Christian evangelicals, it was criticized by gay rights groups, who were angered over Mr. Warren's support for California’s ban on same-sex marriages. The Rev. Rick Warren has created one of the most popular ministries in the country, based at the Saddleback Church in Orange County, Calif. Much of his message has reached an even broader audience outside of his mega-church through his best-selling faith-based book "The Purpose-Driven Life."</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-05</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
