    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.anne_teresa_de_keersmaeker"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">de Keersmaeker, Anne Teresa</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/de_keersmaeker_anne_teresa_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">8</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/d/anne_teresa_de_keersmaeker/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Since the premiere of her striking 1982 dance, "Fase," the Belgian choreographer Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker has remained one of contemporary dance's most important voices. Ms. De Keersmaeker began her career at MUDRA, the school founded by Maurice BÃ©jart. She presented her first piece when she was 20 years old. In 1981, she went to study at New York University's Tisch School of the Arts, and in 1983 she founded her own dance company, Rosas.
In 1999, Anna Kisselgoff, then the chief dance critic of The Times, wrote: "Like a modernist painter who makes his core statement with the near proverbial black square, Ms. De Keersmaeker has felt the need to move on while still remaining true to herself. 'Fase,' completed in 1982, established her Minimalist esthetic. Her choreography used repetitive, reductive movement, but unlike cool American Minimalists, she expressed strong emotions through her rigorous structures. Above all, form generated meaning in her work."</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N59922550749472307653">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">25</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Aug 31, 2009
is expected to become the next prime minister. Yukio Hatoyama Taro Aso became Japan's prime minister in September 2008.  Chosen by the governing Liberal Democratic Party, he was the country's fourth premier in two years. In national elections on Aug. 30, 2009, the Liberal Democrats were roundly defeated by the opposition Democratic Party, and Mr. Aso stepped down as party leader. The Democrats'
Mr. Aso had been trying to put off national elections, which he was required to call by Sept. 10, in part because opinion polls showed that the Liberal Democrats were almost certain to lose. Mr. Aso's hand appeared to be forced, though, by a defeat in a municipal election in Tokyo.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/a/taro_aso/index.html"/>
