  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/44933726032278667943">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Madeleine Korbel Albright, a Czech-born refugee, became the first woman ever to hold the post of United States secretary of state. She served from January 1997 to January 2001, in President Bill Clinton?s second administration, after having been United States representative to the United Nations starting in 1991.
Born Marie Jana Korbelova in Prague, Czechoslovakia, on May 15, 1937, Dr. Albright fled with her family when the Nazis occupied the country, then again when the Communists moved in after the war. She first attended school in Switzerland; her family was granted political asylum in the United States, and they settled in Denver in 1948, when she was 11. Her father, Josef Korbel, who had been a member of the Czech diplomatic corps, was a longtime professor of international relations and dean at the University of Denver. (One of his students was one of her successors as secretary of state, Condoleezza Rice.)</skos:definition>
