  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N55542446246253584953">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/eliot_thomas_stearns_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">4</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">T.S. Eliot, the quiet, gray figure who gave new meaning to English-language poetry, died Jan. 5, 1965 at his home in London. He was 76 years old.
Eliot was an American, born in St. Louis. He moved to England at the beginning of World War I and became wholly identified with Britain, even becoming naturalized in 1927.
Nevertheless, when President Johnson recently awarded the Medal of Freedom to leaders in American literature and public life, Eliot was among those honored. He did not make the trip to the United States, however, to receive the award.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-08-13</nyt:first_use>
