  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/4786901758265418423">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Dec. 2, 2009
Bobby Bowden, the longtime football coach at Florida State University, announced Dec. 1, 2009, that he would retire at age 80. He was the second-winningest coach in major-college football behind Penn State's Joe Paterno. Over 44 years as a head coach â€” beginning at his alma mater, Howard College (now Samford University), and then at West Virginia â€” he won 388 games, behind only the 393 victories recorded by the 83-year-old Paterno.
In his 34 years at Florida State, Bowden transformed a college football outpost into a perennial national power. Under Bowden, the Seminoles won national championships in 1993 and 1999, and played in a bowl game every year since 1982, the longest active streak in the nation. From 1987 to 2000, the Seminoles won 10 or more games and finished in the top five in the Associated Press top 25 poll for 14 consecutive seasons.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
