  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/41222192137574816483">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/anthony_m_kennedy/index.html"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Kennedy, Anthony M</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">48</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: July 1, 2009
Justice Anthony M. Kennedy of the United States Supreme Court is the most important jurist in America. Over the last decade, Justice Kennedy has provided the pivotal swing vote in dozens of cases, particularly since the retirement of Justice Sandra Day O'Connor. As a conservative Republican who sides sometimes with his more conservative colleagues and sometimes with the court's more liberal wing, Justice Kennedy has come to embody a new variation on what Justice William J. Brennan Jr. called the Rule of Five. The Constitution, Justice Brennan liked to say, means whatever five justices say it means. On a deeply polarized court, the Constitution now often means whatever Justice Kennedy says it means.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/kennedy_anthony_m_per"/>
