    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Kirchner, Nestor</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-07-15</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">25</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/kirchner_nestor_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated June 29, 2009
Nestor Kirchner, former president of Argentina and head of the Peronist Party, resigned as party leader on June 29, 2009, one day after he and his supporters suffered a crushing loss in congressional elections. The move was a stunning admission of defeat for a combative and often stubborn politician. It effectively dashed dreams of a political dynasty for Mr. Kirchner and his wife, President Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner, and appeared to put Argentina's presidency up for grabs in the 2011 elections.
Mr. Kirchner ran for the lower house of Congress in a bid to resuscitate the flagging administration of his wife, who has struggled to muster more than a 30 percent approval in opinion polls. But his decision to run in Buenos Aires province, the country's most populated, and effectively make his election a referendum on the couple's leadership backfired when he narrowly lost to a millionaire congressman, Francisco de Narvaez.</skos:definition>
