  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N16252920269965393873">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 9, 2009
William C. Ford Jr. is the executive chairman and scion of the founding family of the Ford Motor Company. The great-grandson of the company's founder, Mr. Ford took over as chairman in 1999 and served five years as its chief executive.
With Detroit on the brink of disaster, Mr. Ford could play a critical role in how the Obama administration decides to assist the companies financially and shape broader energy policies.
Mr. Ford has been Detroit's most vocal environmentalist since becoming the first family member to run Ford since his uncle, Henry Ford II.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-22</nyt:latest_use>
