    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-15</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jobs, Steven P</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Sept. 9, 2009
Perhaps more than anyone, Steven P. Jobs has come to define the global digital culture at the outset of the 21st century. Returning to Apple Computer in 1997 after a decade in exile, Mr. Jobs set out to modernize the company's computers. Then, beginning in 2001, Apple began rolling out an expanding family of entertainment and communications products - most notably the iPod - that have taken the quirky computer maker (now Apple Inc.) far beyond the PC.
His decision not to give his annual Macworld keynote in January 2009 lead to rumors about his health. Forced to respond, Mr. Jobs said that his doctors had recently determined that his visible weight loss was the result of a hormone imbalance that was depleting proteins in his body.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jobs_steven_p_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-18</nyt:first_use>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/57582048450127544933">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-12-13</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BNathan%2C+Judith%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Judith Giuliani, the third wife of former Mayor Rudolph W. Giuliani, has been in his life since May 1999, but it was not until 2007 that she was pushed into the national spotlight. At times, she has appeared uncomfortable there, so much so that Mr. Giuliani's advisers in his presidential campaign for the Republican Party's nomination have sometimes kept her out of public view.
The Giulianis say they met at Club Macanudo, a cigar bar and restaurant in Manhattan, in May 1999. At the time, Mr. Giuliani was halfway into his second term as mayor of New York and was still married to Donna Hanover, with whom he has two children. A year later, he announced his separation from Ms. Hanover, breaking the news -- to her and the public -- via a press conference. Judith Giuliani, who was born on Dec. 16, 1954, and grew up in rural Pennsylvania, was a nursing school graduate and was working at the time in the hospital-sales division of Bristol-Myers Squibb.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">9</nyt:associated_article_count>
