    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">38</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Aug. 21, 2009
Charles, Prince of Wales, born Nov. 14, 1948, is next in line to British throne, the oldest child of Elizabeth II, queen of England.  As a member of the British royal family, he oversees several charitable groups and promotes organic farming, including a food empire in the Duchy of Cornwall.
Members of the British royal family are meant to publicly affect a lack of views on all potentially nonboring subjects. With the exception of Prince Philip, who is prone to unscripted splenetic outbursts, they traditionally steer clear of anything deemed remotely controversial. But Charles has taken it upon himself to speak out on issues like architecture, religion, rain forests, the benefits of homeopathic medicine and the modern tendency, as he sees it, to whine, cast blame on others and file frivolous lawsuits.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-02-23</nyt:first_use>
  </rdf:Description>
