    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BCoulter%2C+Ann%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/c/ann_coulter/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">14</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-04-19</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 10, 2009
Ann Coulter, the ultraconservative, best-selling author and commentator, has crafted a work style that is both provocative and controversial. Her targets are Democrats, liberals and others who suffer her wrath. She was dismissed as contributing editor at the National Review Online after Sept. 11, 2001, for making remarks about Muslims and Arabs that many people found offensive.
Born in 1962, Ms. Coulter grew up in New Canaan, Conn.; her father was a lawyer whose specialty was opposing unions and her mother was a homemaker. She graduated from Cornell and the University of Michigan Law School, where she edited the Michigan Law Review, founded a chapter of a conservative scholars' group, the Federalist Society, and formally embraced the Grateful Dead as a Dead Head.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
