  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/91178019641520997503">
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">35</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.muammar_al-gaddafi"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/q/muammar_el_qaddafi/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 25, 2009
over Lockerbie, Scotland, that killed 270. Pan Am Flight 103 It has been 40 years since President Muammar el-Qaddafi came to power in Libya - and for nearly as long the West has watched his every move. The financier of an eclectic array of guerrilla groups around the globe, he was responsible, according to Western intelligence, for many of the deadliest terrorist attacks in the mid-80s, including the 1988 bombing of
These days Colonel Qaddafi has re-established diplomatic and economic ties throughout Europe. He has done an about-face with regard to Israel. The man who once called for pushing the ''Zionists'' into the sea now advocates the forming of one nation where Jews and Palestinians would live together in peace.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-17</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-07-29</nyt:first_use>
