  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/47963882910264619223">
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2005-03-07</nyt:first_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An enduring star of the American stage, Elaine Stritch, who has said she looked 40 when she was 20, was never an ingenue. She has never even been a leading lady in the conventional sense. Instead, she carved out a singular niche for herself in show business as a worldly, salty, gravel-voiced dame who regularly stole shows from pretty ladies with pretty sopranos.
Born in Detroit to a wealthy family in 1926, she made her Broadway debut in 1947 in the revue "Angels in the Wings." But it was in a 1952 revival of Rodgers and Hart's "Pal Joey" that she caught the public's fancy, performing a mock-striptease in the song "Zip," a parody of Gypsy Rose Lee. Ms. Stritch also appeared memorably as a beleaguered cruise director in the Noel Coward musical "Sail Away" (1961) and in "Company" (1970), which gave her the song (by Stephen Sondheim) with which she will be forever associated, the cynical "Ladies Who Lunch."</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
