    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BBarzani%2C+Massoud%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated July 29, 2009
Massoud Barzani is the president of the semiautonomous Kurdistan region of Iraq, which has long been considered a bastion of stability and democracy in a country ravaged by violence.
He and other Iraq Kurdish leaders have been pushing ahead with a contentious new local constitution, a step that has alarmed Iraqi and American officials who fear that the move poses a new threat to the country's unity.
On July 25, Mr. Barzani was newly empowered as president after winning an estimated 70 percent of the vote in the region's presidential and parliamentary election.</skos:definition>
