  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N32916214085817214773">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Ali Larijani was elected speaker of Iran's parliament on May 28, 2008. Defeated in the 2005 presidential elections by Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, he had been the country's chief nuclear negotiator until he was replaced in October 2007.
Mr. Larijani, although a conservative figure -- he used to run the state's television apparatus -- had been regarded in the West as more moderate on the question of Iran's nuclear ambitions than Mr. Ahmadinejad. In the country's complex governing structure, Mr. Larijani had reported not to the president, but to the country's supreme leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamanei, who has the final say over national security questions. Mr. Larijani's removal was widely seen as an endorsement by the ayatollah of Mr. Ahmadinejad's confrontational approach on the issue.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-09-12</nyt:first_use>
