  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/28820031623086229643">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Audubon, John James</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-12-07</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">John James Audubon (1785-1851) was America's pre-eminent 19th-century naturalist illustrator. He was born in Haiti, the illegitimate son of a French sea captain and his mistress, then taken to France, where as a young man he studied with the neo-Classical painter Jacques-Louis David before escaping to America in 1803 to avoid being drafted in the Napoleonic wars. He married and went into a variety of businesses.
Audubon is best known for his encyclopedic study of the birds of America, painted in New Orleans in the 1820s to make money after he went bankrupt. The drawings were such a success in Europe -- he had no luck selling them to Americans at that time -- that he began a companion study of American mammals. Because of Audubon's poor health in his later years, his son, John Woodhouse Audubon, assisted him, drawing about half of the original illustrations in the 1840s animal series.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BAudubon%2C+John+James%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
