  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N33502201913203794273">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Melvyn I. Weiss is a class-action lawyer who perfected, together with his protege and longtime partner, William S. Lerach, the aggressive pursuit of lawsuits against corporations. The practice that made him extremely wealthy, reviled by much of the business community and ultimately a target for federal prosecutors.
Mr. Weiss, 72, a legal and political powerhouse in New York, appeared to be the final target of a seven-year investigation into the law firm, one that started with the art fraud prosecution of a wealthy ophthalmologist who later admitted serving as a plaintiff-for-hire in several securities lawsuits brought by Milberg Weiss. The probe eventually led to guilty pleas by Mr. Lerach and a number of other members of the firm.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.melvyn_i_weiss"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/weiss_melvyn_i_per"/>
