    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-08-24</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: April 3, 2009
Benjamin Netanyahu appointed Mr. Lieberman as foreign minister in the new coalition government. finished in third place in the Feb. 10, 2009, elections. Prime Minister Avigdor Lieberman is the leader of the right-wing Yisrael Beitenu (Israel Is Our Home) party, which
is at risk not only from outside but also from its own Arab population. Israel Mr. Lieberman is extremely controversial. In 2008, he suggested publicly that Egypt’s president “go to hell.” He wants to require Arab citizens to sign a loyalty oath. His popularity has climbed so steeply that he is a likely power broker with an explosive and apparently resonant political message:</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.avigdor_liberman"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/lieberman_avigdor_per"/>
