    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/e/john_ensign/index.html"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-24</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 2, 2009
John Ensign is a Republican senator from Nevada. He was first elected to the Senate in 2000, after having served in the House for five years. Mr. Ensign led the Republicans' campaign efforts in 2008.
He admitted on June 16, 2009, that he had an extramarital affair with a member of his campaign staff, Cynthia Hampton. Her husband, Douglas Hampton, was one of Mr. Ensign's top aides.
On June 17, Mr. Ensign resigned his position as the fourth-ranking Republican leader in the Senate, but said he had no intention of resigning from his Senate seat. The senator's embarrassment over his misstep seemed to deepen in July, as it was revealed that his wealthy parents gave almost $100,000 to Mrs. Hampton and her family in April 2008.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">17</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/ensign_john_per"/>
