  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/76267885272764408613">
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-02</nyt:latest_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">120</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/favre_brett_per"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 18, 2009
Brett Favre, one of the most iconic, crowd-pleasing players in professional football history, is now the quarterback of the Minnesota Vikings.
For 16 years, Favre was the beloved quarterback of the Green Bay Packers. In March 2008, he told the Packers he was retiring at the age of 38. But months after the tearful farewell, Favre decided to come out of retirement, expressing interest in returning to his old job.
Since Green Bay had prepared itself for a post-Favre future by drafting its current quarterback, Aaron Rodgers, in 2005, the result was a series of awkward missteps out of keeping with Favre's regular guy image, and that culminated in trade that sent Favre to the New York Jets.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.brett_favre"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Favre, Brett</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/f/brett_favre/index.html"/>
