  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N21454096638361190733">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-06</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/edward_steichen/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Edward Steichen, who died in 1973 at 93, was recognized in his lifetime as one of the great photographers of the 20th century. Yet with his penchant for changing directions and playing multiple roles, he bequeathed too many Steichens for easy classification.
in ''pure'' art, fashion and advertising, portraiture, nature, combat, even as a powerful director of photography at the Museum of Modern Art in New York . His detractors suggested that he also became a brand name, famous for being famous.   —    Steichen excelled in all his photographic ventures</skos:definition>
