  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/72866303272528946833">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/n/janet_napolitano/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">49</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-30</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Napolitano, Janet</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/napolitano_janet_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Janet Napolitano, a former governor of Arizona, is the homeland security secretary in the Obama administration.
Ms. Napolitano had called Arizona home since 1983, when she moved to Phoenix to be clerk to Judge Mary Schroeder, then on the Arizona Court of Appeals and now sitting on the federal Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit.
From the clerkship, Ms. Napolitano’s law career took her into private practice, to volunteer work for the state Democratic Party, and in 1991 to Capitol Hill, where she provided legal advice to Anita Hill when she accused Clarence Thomas, a Supreme Court nominee, of sexual harassment.</skos:definition>
