  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/16259606254587162263">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 27, 2009
John Irving, the author of more than a dozen novels, most of them best sellers and printed in more than 30 languages, has both literary stature and worldwide popularity.
Many of his novels deal with an absent parent, a subject that has its origin in the author's life. His parents divorced before he was born, he was adopted by his stepfather and he never met his biological father.
In a question-and-answer session at the 92nd Street Y in 2001, recounted in an article for The New York Times, Mr. Irving was asked how much of his work was autobiographical. He answered that his life was not especially interesting, but ''I can invent more interesting characters than most people I know.'' As he said in an interview, ''In the world of writing about writers, personal experience is, in my view, always overesteemed, and the imagination is almost always devalued.''</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Irving, John</skos:prefLabel>
