    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 2, 2009
The comedian David Letterman is one of the long-reigning kings of late-night talk shows. 
When he was growing up in Indianapolis, he got a Tinker Toy set as a birthday gift and fashioned it into a mike, like the ones used by Steve Allen and Garry Moore on television. He found an idol in Johnny Carson, who took over "Tonight" in 1962.
In the mid-1970s, while he worked on his standup, the former television weatherman dreamed of playing "Tonight." In 1982, Mr. Letterman replaced Tom Snyder to fill the 12:30 A.M. slot for NBC's late-late night talk show, following the ''Tonight'' show.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/letterman_david_per"/>
  </rdf:Description>
