  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/78997568781691286263">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/c/ryan_c_crocker/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.ryan_crocker"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/crocker_ryan_c_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Ryan C. Crocker, a 59-year-old career diplomat who arrived in Baghdad in March 2007 as the American ambassador to Iraq, has consistently advocated a strong and continued presence of American troops in Iraq to ensure stability.
Mr. Crocker, one of the State Department's most experienced Middle East hands and a fluent Arabic speaker, succeeded Zalmay Kalilzad, the colorful Afghan-born diplomat who took an increasingly high profile as he tried to force the fledgling Iraqi government into meeting an American timetable for progress toward political reconciliation. Mr. Crocker has taken a far less public role, concentrating on trying to unify American military and reconstruction efforts. His main public counsel has been for patience -- advice aimed at Congress more than any Iraqi party. Mr. Crocker has repeatedly talked of clocks running at different speeds, meaning that the American desire for quick progress was out of synch with Baghdad's ability to deliver it.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-01-23</nyt:latest_use>
