    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-24</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/edward_i_koch/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.ed_koch"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">26</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated March 3, 2009
By Sam Roberts
Edward I. Koch seemed an unlikely candidate for mayor of New York, much less for the pantheon of great 20th century mayors in which most urban historians have installed him. He was a geeky, relatively obscure congressman, considered too liberal to appeal beyond his Greenwich Village constituency in a Democratic field crowded with political luminaries and an incumbent mayor, Abraham D. Beame.
But Mr. Koch's election in 1977 heralded a transformative epoch. The city emerged stronger from a shibboleth-shattering fiscal crisis. He presided over a robust recovery, leading former Sen. Daniel Patrick Moynihan to declare that the mayor had "given New York City back its morale."</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Koch, Edward I</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
