  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/64643211470779264953">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">6</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/m/sun_myung_moon/index.html"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Oct. 14, 2009
The Rev. Sun Myung Moon is the founder of the Unification Church, perhaps best known for its mass weddings. The movement is centered in South Korea and Japan, but he was also believed to have as many as 30,000 followers in the United States at one time.
Reverend Moon and his wife, Han Hak-ja, are known among his followers as the "true parents of all humankind." According to the church's ''Divine Principle,'' Jesus failed to restore human beings to their position as God's ''perfect children'' because he was crucified before he could marry. Mr. Moon teaches that his mission is to finish Jesus's work by linking married couples and their families to God through him.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
