  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N74564547728355849813">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: June 26, 2009
. Russia 's  Vladimir V. Putin Anna Politkovskaya, an investigative journalist who was a strident critic of the Kremlin, was murdered in 2006. Her killing underlined the shrinking freedom allowed dissenters in Russian society, provoked international outrage and cast a shadow over
Ms. Politkovskaya, who was 48, distinguished herself covering Moscow's war in Chechnya, which she characterized as "state versus group terrorism." She documented torture, mass executions, kidnapping and the sale by Russian soldiers of Chechen corpses to their families for proper Islamic burial, concluding, "What response could one expect but more terrorism, and the recruitment of more resistance fighters?"</skos:definition>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">24</nyt:associated_article_count>
