  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/55235316165375666783">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.james_cayne"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">James E. Cayne, a brash banker who joined Bear Stearns in 1969 and worked his way to the top, was forced to step down as chief executive in January 2008 after the firm recorded the worst losses in its history. He joined a long list of Wall Street executives whose careers were ended or reputations sullied by the mortgage crisis.
Until the mortgage meltdown, the firm had a spectacular run under Mr. Cayne?s decade and a half of leadership, with its stock soaring from $16 to a high of $172 in 2007. With his deep appreciation for the firm?s quirky culture, Mr. Cayne was able to carve out a respectable niche in areas like back office support for mutual and hedge funds while enhancing firm?s expertise in bonds.</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cayne, James E</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">17</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
