  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N32173385723186224333">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/s/nawaz_sharif/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-10-28</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated July 20, 2009
Nawaz Sharif, the leader of the Pakistan Muslim League-N, was the country's prime minister for nine years before Gen. Pervez Musharraf ousted him in a bloodless coup in 1999. He is currently the chief rival to President Asif Ali Zardari, with whom he briefly shared power after they defeated the party of Gen. Pervez Musharraf in parliamentary elections in 2008.
The Bush administration held Mr. Sharif at arm's length because of his close ties to Islamists in Pakistan, but Obama administration have reached out to him in the hope that those ties could be useful in fashioning a response to the growing power of the Taliban and other extremists. The move also reflects heightened concern about whether the deeply unpopular Zardari administration can survive. In March 2009, Mr. Zardari was forced to back down after a court ruling widely seen as the president's attempt to sideline Mr. Sharif led to mass protests in the Pashtun region that is Mr. Sharif's base of support.</skos:definition>
