  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N57026715682751732163">
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/gramm_phil_per"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">9</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.phil_gramm"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Phil Gramm is a former United States senator from Texas who was one of the most effective Congressional fighters in favor of deregulation of the financial markets.
Mr. Gramm, a Republican from Texas, left Capitol Hill in 2002 to work as an investment banker and lobbyist for UBS, a Swiss bank that has been hard hit by the market downturn.
During a 2001 Senate debate over a measure to curb predatory lending, he argued that what some viewed as exploitive, others saw as a gift. ?Some people look at subprime lending and see evil,? he said. ?I look at subprime lending and I see the American dream in action.? He cited how in 1950 his mother, a young nurse working double shifts to support her three children and her disabled husband, was able to acquire his childhood home. Considered something of a risk, she took out a mortgage with relatively high interest rates that he likened to today?s subprime loans.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/g/phil_gramm/index.html"/>
