    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hoover, J Edgar</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/h/j_edgar_hoover/index.html"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-09-14</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Jan. 1, 1895 - May 2, 1972
. His power was a compound of performance and politics, publicity and personality. At the base of it all, however, was an extraordinary record of innovation and modernization in law enforcement -- most of it in the first decade or so of his tenure. Federal Bureau of Investigation Mr. Hoover was the first Director of the
The centralized fingerprint file at the Identification Division (1925) and the crime laboratory (1932) are landmarks in the gradual application of science to police work. The National Police Academy (1935) has trained the leadership elite of local forces throughout the country. Mr. Hoover's recruitment of lawyers and accountants set a world standard of professionalism.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-10-24</nyt:first_use>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.j_edgar_hoover"/>
  </rdf:Description>
