    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated March 31, 2009
. Joseph Bruno A survivor of the old Democratic clubhouse with a lugubrious voice and a Sphinx-like manner, Sheldon Silver is barely known to the general public. Yet he is the longest-serving Democratic speaker in New York history. For years he was one of those “three men in a room,” who wielded life-or-death power over almost every bill in Albany, along with the governor and the Republican Senate leader,
, Mr. Silver has become more influential than ever, as the 2009 budget negotiations showed. He forced Mr. Paterson to raise taxes on the wealthiest New Yorkers, an idea that the governor decried as potentially disastrous. He also turned the clock back to the most secretive budget negotiations the capital has seen in years, casting aside the open government that Mr. Paterson and other Democrats once said would follow the party's sweeping victories in recent state elections. Malcolm A. Smith  and an inexperienced leader of the Senate in  David A. Paterson Working alongside an unpopular governor in</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-09-11</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-12-09</nyt:latest_use>
  </rdf:Description>
