    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">18</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated March 19, 2009
Liam Neeson is 6 feet 4 inches tall, fit and heroic-looking, with a face that can express a kind of beatific innocence. He is still, in his 50s, something of heartthrob. He was Oskar Schindler, the womanizing savior of European Jews during the Holocaust in Steven Spielberg's "Schindler's List" in 1993. And Broadway theatergoers vividly remember his sizzling performance as the seaman, Mat Burke, opposite his future wife, Natasha Richardson, in the Roundabout Theater Company's 1993 production of Eugene O'Neill's ''Anna Christie.''</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
