    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hakim, Abdul Aziz al-</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Aug. 27, 2009
was the leader of the Supreme Iraqi Islamic Council until he died of cancer in August 2009. He had longstanding ties with Iran but was also a supporter of the American invasion of Iraq. Abdul Aziz al-Hakim
. The chief aim of the group was to topple Saddam Hussein. the Supreme Council for Islamic Revolution in Iraq Mr. Hakim fled Iraq in 1982, and while in exile in Iran he helped establish the organization that he would go on to lead, called
Mr. Hakim led the group's militia and fought on the side of Iran in the eight-year war with Iraq. He assumed control of the council shortly after the American invasion in 2003. The group changed its name in 2007, dropping the word "revolution" as part of a bid to reduce perceptions of heavy Iranian influence.</skos:definition>
