  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/61050400431450990363">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Stephon Marbury, a point guard who moved from a playground legend in Brooklyn to a star in the N.B.A., has amassed dazzling highlights and statistics for several professional teams. But questions about his ability to lead a winning team have trailed him along the way.
Despite averaging nearly 20 points and eight assists in his career, Marbury has been regularly criticized by coaches and teammates for his attitude and sometimes selfish play. After playing just one season for Georgia Tech, Marbury was the No. 4 overall pick in the 1996 draft. After a little over two seasons with the Minnesota Timberwolves, he was traded to the New Jersey Nets, who kept him for about two seasons before trading him to the Phoenix Suns. A trade in January 2004 brought him to the New York Knicks, his favorite team growing up.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:search_api_query rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://api.nytimes.com/svc/search/v1/article?query=+nytd_per_facet%3A%5BMarbury%2C+Stephon%5D&amp;rank=newest&amp;fields=abstract,author,body,byline,classifiers_facet,column_facet,date,day_of_week_facet,des_facet,desk_facet,fee,geo_facet,lead_paragraph,material_type_facet,multimedia,nytd_byline,nytd_des_facet,nytd_geo_facet,nytd_lead_paragraph,nytd_org_facet,nytd_per_facet,nytd_section_facet,nytd_title,nytd_works_mentioned_facet,org_facet,page_facet,per_facet,publication_day,publication_month,publication_year,section_page_facet,small_image_height,small_image_url,small_image_width,source_facet,title,url,word_count,works_mentioned_facet</nyt:search_api_query>
