    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">, she had become an unusual fixture in Colombia. Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia At the time Ingrid Betancourt, a politician with Colombian and French citizenship, was kidnapped in 2002 by guerrillas of the
Elected to the National Assembly in 1994 on the strength of a great campaign gimmick — the well-brought-up young woman stood on Bogotá street corners handing out ''condoms against corruption'' — she was swept into the Senate two years later with more votes than any other candidate. In 2002, she was barnstorming the country as a gadfly presidential contender, denouncing the political elite into which she was born.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2007-11-23</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Betancourt, Ingrid</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
