    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.harry_reid"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/reid_harry_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-11-09</nyt:first_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: Oct. 27, 2009
Harry Reid of Nevada, the Senate majority leader, is a tricky figure to pigeonhole or predict, a Democrat who is a Mormon opposed to abortion, and who looks more like a civics teacher than the most powerful person in the Senate.
Mr. Reid is low-key, deferential and somewhat sheepish, qualities that make it easy to misread or underestimate him. He makes an unlikely front man.
In October 2009, Mr. Reid and House speaker Nancy Pelosi worked independently to merge sweeping health care bills produced by two committees in the Senate and three in the House into bills each could squeeze through the respective chambers.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
  </rdf:Description>
