    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/k/kwame_m_kilpatrick/index.html"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">44</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-05-07</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Feb. 6, 2009
He pled to two felony counts of obstruction of justice and no contest to a felony count of assault on a police officer. He agreed to pay restitution to the city of $1 million, to surrender his law license, forfeit his state pension to the city and be barred from elective office for five years, and to serve 120 days in the Wayne County jail, followed by five years’ probation. The other charges were dismissed. On September 4, after Gov. Jennifer M. Granholm had begun holding hearings on whether to remove Mr. Kilpatrick from office, he agreed to resign and plead guilty to obstruction of justice charges. On Aug. 8, 2008, Mr. Kilpatrick was charged with two felony counts of assaulting or obstructing a police officer. The officer, a sheriff’s deputy, was attempting to serve a subpoena. On March 18, 2008, the City Council voted to ask Mr. Kilpatrick to step down, echoing similar calls from the local media and many prominent citizens. On March 23, 2008 Mr. Kilpatrick was charged by the Wayne County prosecutor with misconduct in office, obstruction of justice, conspiracy to commit obstruction of justice and perjury, felonies that could end his political career and send him to prison for as long as 80 years. Mr. Kilpatrick, who is married and has three sons, has apologized to his wife, Carlita, and to the city, but -- before his plea deal in September -- vowed, repeatedly, to stay in office. He defiantly accused the news media of creating his woes, and blamed racism for his troubles. In January 2008, the political crisis arose over the publication of racy text messages from the Mayor to a close female advisor, suggesting they had carried on a romantic affair. The messages also contradicted testimony that Mr. Kilpatrick and his advisor gave under oath last year during a civil trial in which several former police officers accused Mr. Kilpatrick of forcing them out of jobs, in part because their investigations might have uncovered his romance. In addition, documents revealed that Mr. Kilpatrick settled the case (and a second, similar lawsuit) for $8.4 million. Though he swiftly drew controversy, Detroiters looked past talk of partying with exotic dancers, cronyism and a red Lincoln Navigator leased for his family with taxpayers' money. In 2005, voters re-elected Mr. Kilpatrick, who had been among the youngest mayors to run a major American city and whose draw, in part, was his sway over young voters. Mr. Kilpatrick was dubbed the hip-hop mayor, in part for his age, his flashy suits, and the diamond stud in his ear. He began serving his four-month sentence on Oct. 28, 2008, and was released on Feb. 4, 2009. But in 2008 he became embroiled in legal problems stemming from a sex scandal, and on Sept. 4, pleaded guilty to obstruction of justice, agreeing to resign as part of a deal with prosecutors. In 2001, at 31, Kwame M. Kilpatrick, 6-foot-4 and a former college football player, beat an opponent twice his age for mayor of Detroit, a job he once said he had dreamed of since he was a child.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.kwame_kilpatrick"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
