  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N43905337776140378023">
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/i/daniel_k_inouye/index.html"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.daniel_inouye"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/inouye_daniel_k_per"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Adapted from The National Journal's Almanac of American Politics
The largest figure in Hawaii?s public life remains Senator Daniel K. Inouye, who has held elective office here since before Hawaii attained statehood in 1959. Inouye (pronounced in-NO-ay) grew up in Honolulu, the son of Japanese immigrants.
He served in the 442d Regimental Combat Team in World War II in France and Italy, and earned 15 medals and citations.
He was elected to the territorial legislature in 1954, the House in 1959, and the Senate in 1962. He was keynoter at the turbulent 1968 Democratic National Convention, a tenacious member of the Senate Watergate Committee in 1973?74 and the first chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee, in 1976.</skos:definition>
