    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jung, Carl Gustav</skos:prefLabel>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.carl_jung"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated Sept. 23, 2009
, was responsible for popularizing the idea that a person's interior life merited not just attention but dedicated exploration - a notion that has since propelled tens of millions of people into psychotherapy. Sigmund Freud Carl Jung founded the field of analytical psychology and, along with
Whether or not he would have wanted it this way, Jung - who regarded himself as a scientist - is today remembered more as a countercultural icon, a proponent of spirituality outside religion and the ultimate champion of dreamers and seekers everywhere, which has earned him both posthumous respect and posthumous ridicule. Jung's ideas laid the foundation for the widely used Myers-Briggs personality test and influenced the creation of Alcoholics Anonymous. His central tenets - the existence of a collective unconscious and the power of archetypes - have seeped into the larger domain of New Age thinking while remaining more at the fringes of mainstream psychology.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
