  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/57569850276270025913">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Judith Jamison, artistic director of Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater since 1989, announced on February 28, 2008, that she would retire in 2011. Ms. Jamison (pronounced JAM-ih-son) continued the company's development from a home for black dancers who couldn't get jobs in the predominantly dance world into what is arguably the world's most successful modern-dance troupe. Under her leadership, the company built a gleaming new $56 million headquarters at Ninth Avenue and 55th Street in Manhattan.
" by Jennifer Dunning, February 28, 2008. Judith Jamison, Director of the Alvin Ailey Troupe, Announces Retirement --Adapted from " Now 64, she was a very tall, ballet-trained dancer from Philadelphia when Ailey spotted her in a disastrous audition and invited her to join his company in 1965. She was his "gangly girl with no hair," his beauty, Ailey would later say, and his signature piece "Cry," created for her, summed up her qualities. She became a star in her 15 years with the Ailey company, which she left in 1980 to perform on Broadway in "Sophisticated Ladies." With Ailey?s encouragement, she eventually created a modern-dance troupe of her own before returning to become artistic director just weeks after Ailey's death.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jamison_judith_per"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2004-12-03</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jamison, Judith</skos:prefLabel>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
