  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/46489124929117349983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <nyt:associated_article_count rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">13</nyt:associated_article_count>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.james_l_jones"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/jones_james_l_per"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">2</nyt:number_of_variants>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-31</nyt:latest_use>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 7, 2009
Gen. James L. Jones, a former commander of all NATO forces, is President Obama's national security adviser.
General Jones is rarely seen at Mr. Obama's side; neither does he serve as a gateway to the president. He has also kept a low profile with the news media. Inside the Obama administration, the fact that the 6-foot-5 former Marine Corps commandant has left only the faintest of footprints has prompted some early sniping, including the argument that he is not using his position to wield influence or to bring policy debates to resolution.</skos:definition>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/reference/timestopics/people/j/james_l_jones/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2006-09-09</nyt:first_use>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_per"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Jones, James L</skos:prefLabel>
