  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/6393413894186620073">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: March 11, 2009
permanent campaign-style of politicking, Ms. Gillibrand (pronounced JILL-uh-brand) became a ubiquitous and studiously folksy presence at malls and county fairs, racked up $4.6 million in donations — much of it from corporate political action committees — and swept 65 percent of the vote in 2008. Charles E. Schumer She won her first elected position in 2006, defeating a four-term incumbent in a traditionally Republican district that extends from the Hudson Valley flatlands to the mountainous North Country. Then, adopting the  Ms. Gillbrand seems born and bred to politics, a relentless campaigner and fund-raiser, a competitive woman whose friends, unprompted, suggest she might someday soon seek the presidency.  on Jan. 23, 2009, to complete the Senate term of Hillary Rodham Clinton, who has become Secretary of State. The appointment capped a messy two-month period in which many candidates, including Caroline Kennedy, sought the post. Ms. Gillibrand was appointed shortly after Ms. Kennedy withdrew from consideration. Gov. David A. Paterson Kirsten E. Gillibrand was appointed by</skos:definition>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-11-19</nyt:latest_use>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">3</nyt:number_of_variants>
